\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=0\cf0\par
This \b Properties\b0  window allows you to personalize your pointing device settings. Use the menu tree to the left to access the customizable features.\par
\par
\b Using the Properties Window\b0\par
Click on a feature in the menu tree to see a page of settings for that feature. Some features have several pages of settings. Click on the \b +\b0  (plus sign) next to a feature to see all its related pages. The settings will appear in the window to the right of the menu tree. Information about these options and instructions on how to change them will appear here in this information box. You can use the scroll bar to view the contents of the information box.\par
\par
\b Defaults Button\b0\par
The \b Defaults\b0  button returns all the settings on the current page back to their factory-set values.\par
\par
\b OK Button\b0\par
The \b OK\b0  button saves your changes and closes the Properties window.\par
\par
\b Cancel Button\b0\par
The \b Cancel\b0  button closes the Properties window without saving any changes and reverts to all the previous settings.\par
\par
\b Apply Button\b0\par
The \b Apply\b0  button saves your changes, but keeps the Properties window open so you can make more changes.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=1\cf0\par
The \b Enable buttons\b0  check box enables the "mouse" buttons for this pointing device. To configure the action performed when you press a button, select the name of the button from the menu tree. For example, you could define the right button to "go to a web page in your default browser."  Each time you click the right button on your pointing device, your web browser would automatically open and load the specified page.\par
\par
When \b Enable buttons\b0  is not selected, the buttons are disabled and pressing them has no effect; tapping is the only way to click with your pointing device.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=2\cf0\par
\b Scroll item under pointer\b0\par
Select this option if you want to scroll the window or item that is under the pointer. With this option you do not need to click on a window first in order to scroll it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=3\cf0\par
Select an action from the list above to assign it to the selected button. The selected action will occur every time you press the button.\par
\par
If you are unable to select an action from the list, ensure the buttons are enabled. To enable the buttons, perform the following steps:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select \b Buttons\b0  in the menu tree to the left.\par
2.\tab Select the \b Enable buttons\b0  check box.\par
3.\tab Select the button from the \b Buttons\b0  menu tree to return to this page.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=4\cf0\par
This button brings up a configuration dialog box pertaining to the action selected in the list box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=5\cf0\par
The \b Press-to-Select\b0  feature allows you to use tapping and pressing motions to perform "mouse" clicks.\par
\par
Tapping or pressing on the tip of the pointing stick is the same as clicking the left mouse button. However, tapping or pressing can be quicker and more convenient than using the mouse button.\par
\par
"Tapping" means quickly striking and then releasing your finger from the top of the pointing stick. A light, quick tap works best. Very hard or slow taps are less effective. Tap once for a single click, or twice in rapid succession for a double-click.\par
\par
"Pressing" means swiftly pushing down on the top of the pointing stick with your finger. A light, deliberate press works best. Press straight down to simulate clicking and holding the mouse button. Press straight down and then to the side to click-and-drag in any direction. Release the pressure when you want to release the mouse button.\par
\par
Select the \b Enable Press-to-Select\b0  check box to enable this feature.\par
\par
The physical buttons can also be used to click; if you clear this check box, the physical buttons are the only way to click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=6\cf0\par
This menu tree lists the options for your pointing device. To configure an option, click on the entry corresponding to the option.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=7\cf0\par
The \b Pointer Motion\b0  settings pages allow you to specify how the "mouse" pointer on the screen moves in response to your pointing actions.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=8\cf0\par
The \b Constrained Motion\b0  feature allows you to force purely horizontal or vertical motion of the pointer on the screen. This feature is most useful when using a drawing program. You activate Constrained Motion by pressing and holding a specified key while moving the pointer. When the key is pressed, the initial direction of your pointer motion determines the allowed direction of movement for as long as your finger remains on the TouchPad.\par
\par
To enable the Constrained Motion feature:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable constrained motion\b0  check box to enable the options in the list box.\par
2.\tab Choose a key from the list box for activating Constrained Motion.\par
3.\tab Press and hold the selected key whenever you want to constrain pointer motion.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=9\cf0\par
\b Press-to-Select means left click\b0\par
This option produces a left mouse click when you tap or press on the pointing stick. A right mouse click is generally used to display a context pop up menu.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=10\cf0\par
These keys can be used to activate the Constrained Motion mode. Select the key of your choice by clicking on the entry in the list box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=11\cf0\par
The \b EdgeMotion\b0\'99 feature is designed to simplify long-distance pointer motions. When your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad, the pointer will continue to move in the same direction until you lift your finger. Select one of the EdgeMotion options.\par
\par
The Virtual Scrolling feature also supports EdgeMotion. To control EdgeMotion during scrolling, select \b Long Distance Scrolling\b0  under \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  in the menu tree.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=12\cf0\par
\b Perform EdgeMotion only when dragging\b0\par
When this option is selected, EdgeMotion occurs only during a tap-and-drag gesture. EdgeMotion will not occur during ordinary pointer movement. Tapping must be enabled for this feature to work.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=13\cf0\par
\b Perform EdgeMotion when dragging and pointing\b0\par
When this option is selected, EdgeMotion occurs whenever your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=14\cf0\par
\b No EdgeMotion\b0\par
To move the pointer a long distance without EdgeMotion, you must make repeated finger strokes on the pad or increase the pointer motion speed in the Mouse Control Panel.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=15\cf0\par
By selecting the \b Enable Tap Zones\b0  check box, you can configure each of the four corners of the TouchPad surface to act as different buttons when tapped. These corner regions are called \b Tap Zones\b0 .\par
\par
Tapping in the center of the TouchPad always produces a left click.\par
\par
If you clear the \b Enable Tap Zones\b0  check box, then a tap anywhere on the TouchPad produces a left click.\par
\par
A variety of actions can be assigned to the tap zones.\par
\par
To enable Tap Zones:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Ensure that \b Enable Tapping\b0  is selected on the \b Tapping\b0  page.\par
2.\tab Select the \b Enable Tap Zones\b0  check box above.\par
3.\tab Select the name of a Tap Zone in the menu tree to assign an action to that zone.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=16\cf0\par
Select an action for taps in the bottom left corner zone of your TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Middle Click\b0 , then a tap in the bottom left corner of the TouchPad surface performs the same action as a middle click. Select \b No Zone\b0  to disable this \b tap zone\b0  (then taps in the zone act like a left click, just as on the rest of the pad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=17\cf0\par
Select an action for taps in the top left corner zone of your TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Middle Click\b0 , then a tap in the top left corner of the TouchPad surface performs the same action as a middle click. Select \b No Zone\b0  to disable this \b tap zone\b0  (then taps in the zone act like a left click, just as on the rest of the pad).\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=18\cf0\par
Select an action for taps in the top right corner zone of your TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Right Click\b0 , then a tap in the top right corner of the TouchPad surface performs the same action as pressing the right mouse or TouchPad button. Select \b No Zone\b0  to disable this \b tap zone\b0  (then taps in the zone act like a left click, just as on the rest of the pad).\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=19\cf0\par
Select an action for taps in the bottom right corner zone of your TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Right Click\b0 , then a tap in the bottom right corner of the TouchPad surface performs the same action as pressing the right mouse or TouchPad button. Select \b No Zone\b0  to disable this \b tap zone\b0  (then taps in the zone act like a left click, just as on the rest of the pad).\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=20\cf0\par
Press this button to see the Properties dialog for the selected action.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=21\cf0\par
The diagram above represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the active Tap Zone regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger's position. If your finger is in an active Tap Zone region on the TouchPad, the corresponding Tap Zone area on the diagram will turn a solid color. You can make the Tap Zone regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the corners of the cross-hatched areas.\par
\par
If you have difficulty triggering a Tap Zone action, try making the Tap Zone region larger. If a Tap Zone action triggers too easily, try making the Tap Zone region smaller.\par
\par
If the Tap Zones are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that \b Tapping\b0  and \b Tap Zones\b0  are selected.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=22\cf0\par
This section allows you to customize your TouchPad buttons.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=23\cf0\par
\b Defaults\b0\par
Returns all settings on this page to their default values.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=24\cf0\par
EdgeMotion speed can be configured to be pressure-sensitive or fixed.\par
\par
\b Control speed with finger pressure\b0\par
Select this option if you want the EdgeMotion speed to increase as you press your finger harder on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
\b Fixed speed\b0\par
Select this option to make the EdgeMotion speed the same all the time.\par
\par
\b Adjust EdgeMotion Speed\b0\par
This slider controls the speed of the EdgeMotion when the \b Fixed speed\b0  option is selected. You can choose the EdgeMotion speed by adjusting the slider between \b Slow\b0  and \b Fast\b0 .\par
\par
If the controls on this page are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that EdgeMotion is enabled. To enable EdgeMotion, perform the following steps:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select \b EdgeMotion\b0  in the menu tree to the left.\par
2.\tab Select one of the \b Perform EdgeMotion\b0  options.\par
3.\tab Select \b EdgeMotion Speed\b0  from the menu tree to return to this page.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=25\cf0\par
When the \b Control speed with finger pressure\b0  option is selected, EdgeMotion speed is determined by how hard you press your finger on the TouchPad.\par
\par
When the \b Fixed speed\b0  option is selected, the pointer moves at the specified speed when the finger is in the EdgeMotion region.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=26\cf0\par
Select this check box for constant EdgeMotion speed.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=27\cf0\par
Tapping on the surface of the TouchPad is the same as clicking the left TouchPad "mouse" button. However, tapping is usually quicker and more convenient than using the mouse button.\par
\par
"Tapping" means quickly touching and then lifting your finger off the TouchPad surface. A light, quick tap works best. Very hard or slow taps are less effective. Tap once for a single click, or twice in rapid succession for a double-click.\par
\par
Select the \b Enable Tapping\b0  check box to enable this feature. The TouchPad buttons can also be used to click. If you clear this check box, the TouchPad buttons are the only way to click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=28\cf0\par
\b Virtual Scrolling Tutorial\b0\par
This simple maze will help you learn how to use the Virtual Scrolling feature.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=29\cf0\par
When the \b Tap to Click\b0  check box is not selected, you must press the actual TouchPad buttons to produce clicks.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=30\cf0\par
\b Tap again and hold to drag\b0\par
Select this check box to drag objects and selections by tapping the TouchPad. To drag, tap twice on the object and keep your finger down on the TouchPad on the second tap. Move the selected object by sliding your finger across the TouchPad surface and lift your finger to drop the object. This action produces the same effect as pressing a mouse button, moving the mouse, and releasing the mouse button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=31\cf0\par
\b Locking drags\b0\par
The \b Locking drags\b0  feature allows objects to be dragged long distances across the screen using several finger strokes. When \b Locking drags\b0  is selected, a drag action persists after you lift your finger from the TouchPad. Tap the TouchPad again or click on any button to drop the object and end the drag. To use \b Locking drags\b0 , the \b Tap again and hold to drag\b0  feature must also be selected.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=32\cf0\par
The \b PalmCheck\b0\'99 feature guards against operating the TouchPad with accidental contact. PalmCheck allows the TouchPad to recognize when your palm is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing. This will help to prevent unwanted pointer movement or clicks.\par
\par
If the TouchPad exhibits undesired pointer movement or clicks, increase the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider to the right toward \b Maximum\b0 . If the TouchPad misses intended motions or taps, decrease the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider to the left toward \b Minimum\b0 . You are more likely to experience missed motions or taps if the slider is in the red zone.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=33\cf0\par
The \b Touch Sensitivity\b0  slider allows you to adjust how much finger pressure must be applied before the TouchPad responds.\par
\par
If your pointer is moving or clicking erratically, or you work in an area with high humidity, decrease sensitivity by moving the slider to the right toward \b Heavy Touch\b0 .\par
\par
If you have to press too hard to operate the TouchPad or if the TouchPad does not always respond to your taps, increase sensitivity by moving the slider to the left toward \b Light Touch\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=34\cf0\par
\b Scroll selected item\b0\par
Select this option if you want to scroll the currently active window or item. This is usually the last window, part of a window, or item that you clicked on.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=35\cf0\par
Select the \b Enable EdgeMotion when scrolling\b0  check box if you want scrolling to continue when your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=36\cf0\par
\b Press-to-Select means right click\b0\par
This option produces a right mouse click when you tap or press on the pointing stick. A right mouse click is generally used to display a context pop up menu.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=37\cf0\par
The \b Coasting\b0  feature helps you to scroll easily through long documents. With Coasting, Virtual Scrolling continues after you have lifted your finger from the TouchPad. To use Coasting, move and lift your finger in one smooth motion. To stop the Coasting motion, place your finger back on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=38\cf0\par
\b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0\par
Select this check box to enable horizontal Virtual Scrolling on windows that have horizontal scroll bars. Sliding your finger back and forth along the lower edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=39\cf0\par
\b Enable vertical scrolling\b0\par
Select this check box to enable vertical Virtual Scrolling. Sliding your finger up and down the right edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item vertically.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=40\cf0\par
The diagram above represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the Virtual Scrolling regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger's position. If your finger is in an active Virtual Scrolling region on the TouchPad, the corresponding Virtual Scrolling area on the diagram will turn a solid color. You can make the Virtual Scrolling regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the edge of the cross-hatched area.\par
\par
If you have difficulty triggering Virtual Scrolling, try making the Virtual Scrolling region larger by moving the edges of the cross-hatched area inward. If you find that Virtual Scrolling triggers too easily, try making the Virtual Scrolling region smaller.\par
\par
If the Virtual Scrolling regions are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that Virtual Scrolling is enabled. To enable Virtual Scrolling, perform the following steps:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  in the menu tree to the left.\par
2.\tab Select the desired \b Enable Scrolling\b0  check boxes.\par
3.\tab Select \b Scrolling Region\b0  from the menu tree to return to this page.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=41\cf0\par
\b Practice Button\b0\par
Click the \b Practice\b0  button to run the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  tutorial. This simple maze will help you learn how to use Virtual Scrolling.\par
\par
NOTE: Ensure that the \b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0  and \b Enable vertical scrolling\b0  check boxes are selected.\par
\par
Survey the maze before you begin to locate the beginning and ending points. You cannot see the entire maze because it is larger than the window that displays it. Use Virtual Scrolling to move your view of the maze in the window. Hold down the Ctrl key and use Virtual Scrolling to zoom the maze larger or smaller.\par
\par
A yellow dot in the upper left corner of the maze marks the entrance to the maze. A blue dot on the right side of the maze marks the exit.\par
\par
To Begin:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Click on the red square in the yellow dot to begin.\par
2.\tab Use the Arrow keys or tap your TouchPad to navigate through the maze.\par
3.\tab Use Virtual Scrolling to move your view of the maze.\par
4.\tab Click the close button on the Virtual Scrolling Practice window after you reach the blue dot in the lower right corner.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=42\cf0\par
This slider allows you to control the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  speed. Drag the slider to the right for faster scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=43\cf0\par
Use this section to customize the pointer motion. Select help on specific areas for more information.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=44\cf0\par
\b Press-to-Select and drag\b0\par
Select this option if you want pressing to perform a select and drag, and tapping to perform just a selection.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=45\cf0\par
The \b Slow Motion\b0  feature allows you to reduce the speed of the pointer to improve pointing accuracy for fine positioning. You activate Slow Motion by pressing and holding a specified key while moving the pointer.\par
\par
To enable the Slow Motion feature:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable slow motion\b0  check box to enable the options in the list box.\par
2.\tab Choose a key from the list box for activating Slow Motion.\par
3.\tab Press and hold the selected key whenever you want Slow Motion.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=46\cf0\par
This feature automatically moves the pointer to the Default Button when a dialog box appears on your screen.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=47\cf0\par
At times you may want to restrict the pointer to move only horizontally or only vertically. You can specify a constrained pointer key, then press and hold this key as you move the pointer. The initial pointer direction when holding this key determines whether the pointer is constrained to move only horizontally or only vertically.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=49\cf0\par
Selecting the \b Fast\b0  pointer refresh rate option generally produces very smooth pointer motion. However, if your pointer appears unsteady or flickers on the screen, try selecting the \b Slow\b0  pointer refresh rate.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=50\cf0\par
The Synaptics TouchPad properties pages allow you to configure your TouchPad. These pages are located in the Mouse Properties Control Panel.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=51\cf0\par
Click here to run your Internet browser and to go directly to this link.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=52\cf0\par
Panning means to scroll continuously. You can pan horizontally, vertically, or diagonally in both directions!\par
Note that panning only works in applications that support the Microsoft Intellimouse and that many of those applications limit the directions that you can pan in (Examples: Microsoft Word only lets you pan vertically;  Microsoft Excel will let you pan vertically and horizontally, but not diagonally).\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=53\cf0\par
Zooming means to change the magnification of your document. Zoom in to increase the magnification and zoom out to decrease the magnification.\par
\par
Note that zooming only works in applications that support the Microsoft Intellimouse.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=54\cf0\par
This action automatically moves the pointer to the Start button in the task bar and opens the start menu.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=55\cf0\par
\b Jump to the Menu of the Current Window\b0\par
This action automatically moves the pointer to the leftmost entry in the current window's menu (usually the "File" menu), and opens it.\par
This feature might not work with some windows that have non-standard menus.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=56\cf0\par
This action minimizes the current window.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=57\cf0\par
This action maximizes the current window so that it covers the entire screen. If the current window is already maximized, then this action restores it to its original size and position.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=58\cf0\par
This action restores a minimized or a maximized window to its original size and position.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=59\cf0\par
This action automatically runs a program that you specify. After selecting this action you can choose the program to run by clicking on the Properties button beside the combo box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=60\cf0\par
This action allows you to use the entire TouchPad surface to control Virtual Scrolling\v Scroll_Through_A_Document_without_Using_Scroll_Bars>main\v0 . To resume normal TouchPad operation, simply perform the associated gesture a second time.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=61\cf0\par
If the active application is a web browser, this action visits the previous page in the browser\rquote s history buffer.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=62\cf0\par
If the active application is a web browser, this action visits the next page in the browser\rquote s history buffer.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=63\cf0\par
This action starts the default web browser and visits a web page. After selecting this action you can choose web page to visit by clicking on the Properties button beside the combo box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=64\cf0\par
If the active application is a web browser, this action causes the current web page to be reloaded from its source.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=65\cf0\par
If the active application is a web browser, this action causes the browser to stop loading the current web page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=66\cf0\par
Placing two fingers on the TouchPad surface and moving them in a particular direction is called a two-finger \b Gesture\b0 . Your TouchPad can detect such movements in each of the eight compass points. This page allows you to customize the action the TouchPad performs when a \b Gesture\b0  is detected.\par
\par
To enable \b Gesture\b0  processing, check the box labeled \b Enable\b0 . Then choose an action from the drop-down list for each \b Gesture\b0  direction.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=67\cf0\par
Select the action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the upper left region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=68\cf0\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up Key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=69\cf0\par
Select the action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the upper region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=70\cf0\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=80\cf0\par
Select an action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the upper left region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=81\cf0\par
Select an action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the left region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=82\cf0\par
Select an action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the lower right region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=83\cf0\par
Select an action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the lower region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=84\cf0\par
Select an action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the lower left region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=85\cf0\par
Select an action to be performed for a two-finger \b Gesture\b0  in the left region of the TouchPad.\par
\par
For example, if you select \b Press the Page Up key\b0 , then gesturing in this manner performs the same action as pressing the Page Up key on the keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=86\cf0\par
\b Record and Play a Keyboard Macro\b0\par
\par
This action plays a pre-recorded sequence of keystrokes. After selecting this action you can record the sequence of keystrokes to play.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=87\cf0\par
\b Keyboard Macro Recording Dialog\b0\par
This dialog allows you to record a sequence of keystrokes for subsequent playback. Such a recorded keystroke sequence is often called a keyboard \i macro\i0 .\par
\par
To begin recording a macro, press the \b Start\b0  button. The \b Stop\b0  button will then be enabled and the \b Start\b0  button disabled indicating that recording is in progress. Since all keystrokes will be intercepted during recording, the only way to exit from the recording mode is to press the Stop button using the mouse.\par
\par
When not recording, the \b Clear\b0  button can be used to empty the recorded keystroke buffer. When recording, this button serves the function of the backspace key. Pressing the \b Back\b0  button removes the last entry from the recorded keystroke buffer. Note that during recording, the \b Back\b0  button must be pressed with the mouse.\par
\par
As they are recorded, keystrokes also appear in the recording dialog\rquote s text window. Alphabetic, numeric, and punctuation keys appear in the text window just as they would appear in a typed document. Non-printable keys are represented in the text window by mnemonic tags enclosed in braces.\par
\par
To make the text window easier to read, most keys are only placed into the text window as they are pressed. However the mode keys (such as \b Shift\b0 , \b Ctrl\b0  and \b Alt\b0 ) are entered into the text window both when they are pressed and when they are released. This makes it possible to locate keyboard mode changes by \ldblquote reading\rdblquote  the text window.\par
\par
Please note that the text window is not directly editable. For instance, it is not possible to insert a key in the middle of a previously recorded sequence. To do this, you must clear the recorded buffer and re-record the desired keystroke sequence.\par
\par
Once a recording is completed, press the \b OK\b0  button to save or the \b Cancel\b0  button to discard your recording. Pressing either button will exit the dialog.\par
\par
