\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=142\cf0\par
This \b Properties\b0  window allows you to personalize your pointing device settings. Use the menu tree to the left to access the customizable features.\par
\par
\b Using the Properties Window\b0\par
Click on a feature in the menu tree to see a page of settings for that feature. Some features have several pages of settings. Click on the \b +\b0  (plus sign) next to a feature to see all its related pages. The settings will appear in the window to the right of the menu tree. Information about these options and instructions on how to change them will appear here in this information box. You can use the scroll bar to view the contents of the information box.\par
\par
\b Restore All Defaults Button\b0\par
The \b Restore All\b0  \b Defaults\b0  button returns all the settings on all pages back to their factory-set values.\par
\par
\b Restore Page Defaults Button\b0\par
The \b Restore Page\b0  \b Defaults\b0  button returns all the settings on the current page back to their factory-set values.\par
\par
\b OK Button\b0\par
The \b OK\b0  button saves your changes and closes the Properties window.\par
\par
\b Cancel Button\b0\par
The \b Cancel\b0  button closes the Properties window without saving any changes and reverts to all the previous settings.\par
\par
\b Apply Button\b0\par
The \b Apply\b0  button saves your changes, but keeps the Properties window open so you can make more changes.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=143\cf0\par
The \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  feature allows you to scroll Web pages, documents, and other items with a TouchPad. Just place your finger in the Scroll Zone-the outer edge of the TouchPad-and slide your finger around in circles. A small ridge embossed in the surface of the TouchPad helps guide your finger. However, you don\rquote t have to confine yourself to the outer area once you\rquote ve started to scroll. Just starting in the Scroll Zone is enough-you can allow your finger to move towards the center of the TouchPad and scroll in tiny circles in the middle if you prefer. To stop scrolling, simply lift your finger up.\par
\par
Virtual Scrolling options include: vertical scrolling (down and up), vertical scrolling and horizontal scrolling (down, up, right, and left), scrolling audio (sound effects), or no scrolling at all.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=144\cf0\par
\b Enable Vertical Scrolling\b0\par
Select this option to be able to scroll down or up.\par
\par
To scroll down, place your finger anywhere in the Scroll Zone and move your finger in clockwise circles.\par
\par
To scroll up, place your finger anywhere in the Scroll Zone and move your finger in counter-clockwise circles.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=145\cf0\par
\b Enable Vertical and Horizontal Scrolling\b0\par
Select this option to be able to scroll down, up, right, or left. The right side of the ScrollZone controls vertical scrolling. The bottom part of the Scroll controls horizontal scrolling.\par
\par
To scroll down, place your finger in the right part of the Scroll Zone and move your finger in clockwise circles.\par
\par
To scroll up, place your finger in the right part of the Scroll Zone and move your finger in counter-clockwise circles.\par
\par
To scroll right, place your finger in the lower part of the Scroll Zone and move your finger in counter-clockwise circles.\par
\par
To scroll left, place your finger in the lower part of the Scroll Zone and move your finger in clockwise circles.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=146\cf0\par
\b Enable scrolling audio\b0\par
Select this option for sound effects to accompany your scrolling actions.\par
\par
NOTE: You must also select \b Vertical Scrolling\b0  or \b Vertical and Horizontal Scrolling\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=147\cf0\par
\b Disable scrolling\b0\par
If you do not want to use any of the Virtual Scrolling features, select \b Disable scrolling\b0 . With this option selected, the Scroll Zone can still be used for pointing and tapping rather than scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=148\cf0\par
\b Illumination Options\b0\par
The LuxPad pointing device has a variety of illumination options. Select the \b Enable Illumination\b0  check box to personalize your LuxPad's light effects.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=149\cf0\par
\b Brightness\b0\par
You can select different levels of brightness for when your notebook computer is plugged into a power source and for when it is running on battery power. Selecting different settings is useful if extending battery life is extremely critical to you.\par
\par
The brightness options for \b External Power\b0  and \b Battery\b0  are \b Low\b0 , \b Medium\b0 , and \b High\b0 .\par
\par
If the options on this page are dimmed and unavailable for use, you can enable them by clicking \b Illumination Options\b0  in the menu tree to the left. Click the \b Enable Illumination\b0  check box to select it and then return to the \b Brightness\b0  page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=150\cf0\par
\b Illumination Time\b0\par
The \b Illumination Time\b0  page provides a variety of light-effect options.\par
\par
Select the \b Always On\b0  check box if you want your LuxPad to glow continuously after you have logged on.\par
\par
The \b Illumination Time After Log On\b0  and the \b Illuminate When Touched\b0  options are only available when the \b Always On\b0  check box is clear. If there is a check mark in the \b Always On\b0  check box, you can click on it to clear it.\par
\par
The \b Illumination Time After Log On\b0  slide bar allows you to specify how long you want your LuxPad to glow after you have logged on. Move the slider to the left for a shorter amount of time or to the right for a longer duration.\par
\par
Select the \b Illuminate When Touched\b0  check box if you want your LuxPad to glow when your finger is on the LuxPad's surface. The LuxPad will glow each time you touch it.\par
\par
If the options on this page are dimmed and unavailable for use, you can enable them by clicking \b Illumination Options\b0  in the menu tree to the left. Click the \b Enable Illumination\b0  check box to select it and then return to the \b Illumination Time\b0  page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=151\cf0\par
To use the pointing stick or TouchPad to move the cursor, select the appropriate \b Enable\b0  option.\par
\par
You can enable both the pointing stick and the TouchPad. If you do, you can touch either one to move the cursor.\par
\par
To use the pointing stick buttons, TouchPad buttons, or both, select the appropriate \b Enable\b0  options.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=152\cf0\par
Select the \b Disable\b0  option for the device, or device buttons, that you do not wish to use; this will prevent you from accidentally activating them when typing or pointing.\par
\par
Note: You cannot disable both the pointing stick and the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=153\cf0\par
To change the action assigned to pressing a \b Left Button\b0 , \b Both Buttons\b0 , or \b Right Button\b0 , click the down arrow in the drop-down list to display the options. Select the desired action.\par
\par
If more information is required for the selected action, the \b Configure\b0  button will become enabled.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=154\cf0\par
If more information is required for the action selected in the drop-down list, the \b Configure\b0  button will become enabled. Click the \b Configure\b0  button and enter the requested information in the dialog box that displays. Click the \b OK\b0  button to save your setting and close the dialog box.\par
\par
You can view or change your settings at any time by clicking the \b Configure\b0  button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=155\cf0\par
To restore the default settings for all the items in the window, click this button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=156\cf0\par
To use a finger tap to perform a primary click, select the \b Tap to Click\b0  check box. A finger tap is a quick down-up motion.\par
\par
When this check box is clear, the only way to perform a primary click is to press a button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=157\cf0\par
To drag objects without pressing a TouchPad button, select the \b Taps and Drags\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use this feature, tap on an object to select it and quickly put your finger back down on the TouchPad and slide your finger in the direction you wish to drag the object. Lift your finger off the TouchPad to release the object.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=158\cf0\par
To drag objects across the screen using several finger strokes, select the \b Locking Drags\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use locking drags, tap on an object to select it. Use as many finger strokes on the TouchPad as necessary to move the object to the desired location. You don\rquote t have to keep your finger down. The selected object is not released until you tap on the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=159\cf0\par
You can use this slider to adjust how much finger pressure must be applied for the TouchPad to respond. If you experience erratic pointer movement, move the slider to the right.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=160\cf0\par
To help prevent unwanted pointer movement or clicks while typing, select the \b Enable PalmCheck\b0  check box.\par
\par
This feature helps the TouchPad recognize when your hand is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=161\cf0\par
To adjust PalmCheck sensitivity, use this slider.\par
\par
If the TouchPad exhibits undesired pointer movement or clicks, increase the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider to the right. However, if you move the slider to the far right, near the \b Maximum\b0  setting, you are more likely to experience missed taps or motions.\par
\par
If the TouchPad misses intended taps or motions, decrease the PalmCheck sensitivity by moving the slider to the left.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=162\cf0\par
EdgeMotion allows the pointer to continue to move when you reach the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
Without EdgeMotion, you must make several finger strokes on the TouchPad or increase the pointer motion speed on the \b Pointer Options\b0  tab to move the pointer long distances easily.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=163\cf0\par
To use \i EdgeMotion\i0  for drag gestures (tap-and-drag and locking drags), select the \b When Dragging\b0  check box.\par
\par
EdgeMotion allows the pointer to continue to move when you reach the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
With this option selected, EdgeMotion will not occur during ordinary pointer movement.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=164\cf0\par
To use \i EdgeMotion\i0  whenever your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad, select the \b When Pointing\b0  check box.\par
\par
EdgeMotion allows the pointer to continue to move when you reach the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=165\cf0\par
To use \i EdgeMotion\i0  when using the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  feature, select the \b When Scrolling\b0  check box.\par
\par
EdgeMotion allows the pointer to continue to move when you reach the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=166\cf0\par
This diagram represents the surface of the TouchPad. The crosshatched area on the diagram shows the EdgeMotion regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger\rquote s position. If your finger is in an active EdgeMotion region on the TouchPad, the corresponding EdgeMotion area on the diagram will turn a solid color. You can make the EdgeMotion regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the edge of the crosshatched area.\par
\par
If you have difficulty activating EdgeMotion, try making the EdgeMotion region larger by moving the edges of the crosshatched area inward. If you find that EdgeMotion triggers too easily, try making the EdgeMotion region smaller.\par
\par
If the EdgeMotion regions are gray and cannot be adjusted, select one of the EdgeMotion options and then you can resize the regions.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=167\cf0\par
To use pressure-sensitive speed, select this check box. With this option selected, pressing harder will make the pointer move faster.\par
\par
For constant speed, clear the \b Control EdgeMotion speed with finger pressure\b0  check box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=168\cf0\par
To adjust EdgeMotion speed when the \b Control EdgeMotion speed with finger pressure\b0  check box is clear, use this slider. Drag the slider to the right for faster EdgeMotion or to the left for slower EdgeMotion.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=169\cf0\par
To configure a Tap Zone to perform an action when tapped, select the \b Enable Tap Zones\b0  check box. Each corner of the TouchPad surface can be configured as a Tap Zone.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=170\cf0\par
To assign an action to a Tap Zone, select an option from the drop-down list. If more information is required for the selected action, the \b Configure\b0  button will become enabled.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=171\cf0\par
This diagram represents the surface of the TouchPad. The crosshatched areas on the diagram show the active Tap Zone regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger\rquote s position. If your finger is in an active Tap Zone region on the TouchPad, the corresponding Tap Zone area on the diagram will turn a solid color. You can make the Tap Zone regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the corners of the crosshatched areas.\par
\par
If you have difficulty triggering a Tap Zone action, try making the Tap Zone region larger. If a Tap Zone action triggers too easily, try making the Tap Zone region smaller.\par
\par
If the Tap Zones are gray and cannot be adjusted, select the \b Enable Tap Zone\b0  check box and then you can resize them.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=172\cf0\par
To use a swipe gesture to perform the function of a \b Forward\b0  or \b Back\b0  arrow button in a Web browser window, select the \b Enable back/forward gestures\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use a forward or back gesture, place your finger along the upper edge of the TouchPad and move your finger to the right or left. Moving your finger to the left is like clicking the back arrow button. Moving your finger to the right is like clicking the forward arrow button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=173\cf0\par
To use the Virtual Scrolling features, select the \b Enable Scrolling\b0  check box.\par
\par
With Virtual Scrolling enabled, sliding your finger up and down the right edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item vertically. Sliding your finger across the lower edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=174\cf0\par
To display the Virtual Scrolling options and adjust your settings, click this button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=175\cf0\par
To scroll the active window or item, select the \b Scroll selected item\b0  option. The active item is usually the last window, part of a window, or item on which you clicked.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=176\cf0\par
To scroll an item that is under the pointer in an active application, select this option. This option allows you to scroll the item under the pointer without clicking on the item first.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=177\cf0\par
To scroll easily through long documents, select the \b Enable coasting\b0  check box. With coasting, Virtual Scrolling continues after you have lifted your finger off the TouchPad.\par
\par
To use coasting, move and lift your finger in one smooth horizontal or vertical motion. To stop coasting, touch the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=178\cf0\par
This diagram represents the surface of the TouchPad. The crosshatched area on the diagram shows the Virtual Scrolling regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger\rquote s position. If your finger is in an active Virtual Scrolling region on the TouchPad, the corresponding Virtual Scrolling area on the diagram will turn a solid color. You can make the Virtual Scrolling regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the edge of the crosshatched area.\par
\par
If you have difficulty triggering Virtual Scrolling, try making the Virtual Scrolling region larger by moving the edges of the crosshatched area inward. If you find that Virtual Scrolling triggers too easily, try making the Virtual Scrolling region smaller.\par
\par
If the Virtual Scrolling regions are gray and cannot be adjusted, select a Virtual Scrolling option and then you can resize the regions.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=179\cf0\par
To run the Virtual Scrolling tutorial, click the \b Practice\b0  button. This simple maze will help you learn how to use Virtual Scrolling.\par
\par
Note: Ensure that both the \b Horizontal Scrolling\b0  and \b Vertical Scrolling\b0  check boxes are selected.\par
\par
Survey the maze before you begin to locate the beginning and ending points. You cannot see the entire maze because it is larger than the window that displays it. Use Virtual Scrolling to move your view of the maze in the window. Hold down the \b Ctrl\b0  key and use Virtual Scrolling to zoom the maze larger or smaller.\par
\par
A yellow dot in the upper left corner of the maze marks the entrance to the maze. A blue dot on the right side of the maze marks the exit.\par
\par
To Begin:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Click on the red square in the yellow dot to begin.\par
2.\tab Use the \b Arrow\b0  keys or tap your TouchPad to navigate through the maze.\par
3.\tab Use Virtual Scrolling to move your view of the maze.\par
4.\tab Click the \b Close\b0  button on the \b Virtual Scrolling Practice\b0  window after you reach the blue dot in the lower right corner.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=180\cf0\par
To use Virtual Scrolling on windows that have horizontal scroll bars, select the \b Horizontal Scrolling\b0  check box. Sliding your finger back and forth along the lower edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=181\cf0\par
To use Virtual Scrolling on windows that have vertical scroll bars, select the \b Vertical Scrolling\b0  check box. Sliding your finger up and down the right edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item vertically.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=182\cf0\par
This slider allows you to control the Virtual Scrolling speed. Drag the slider to the right for faster scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=183\cf0\par
To use tapping and pressing motions to perform a specified action, select the \b Enable Press-to-Select\b0  check box. Actions are assigned in the \b Tapping Action\b0  drop-down list.\par
\par
\i Tapping\i0  is a rapid down-up finger motion on the top of the pointing stick. A light, quick tap works best. Very hard or slow taps are less effective. Tap once for a single click, or twice in rapid succession for a double-click.\par
\par
\i Pressing\i0  means swiftly pushing down on the top of the pointing stick with your finger. A light, deliberate press works best. Press straight down to simulate clicking and holding a mouse button. Press straight down and then to the side to click-and-drag in any direction. Lift your finger to release the object being dragged.\par
\par
When the \b Enable Press-to-Select\b0  check box is clear, you must press a button to perform a primary click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=184\cf0\par
To help prevent unwanted clicks while typing, select this check box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=185\cf0\par
To assign an action to tapping on the pointing stick, select an option from the \b Tapping Action\b0  drop-down list.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=186\cf0\par
To adjust how quickly you must tap the pointing stick for clicks and double-clicks, move this slider.\par
\par
If you set the pace too slow, you might notice unintended clicks, a pointer delay, or a dragging delay. However, if you set the pace too fast, you might notice that it is hard to use Press-to-Select.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=187\cf0\par
To enable Virtual Scrolling, select the \b Enable Scrolling\b0  check box. With this option selected, the pointing stick can only be used for horizontal or vertical Virtual Scrolling. You must use the TouchPad or an external mouse to move the cursor.\par
\par
To use Virtual Scrolling, press the pointing stick in the direction you wish to scroll. To stop scrolling, lift your finger off the pointing stick.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=188\cf0\par
To adjust the speed of \b Virtual Scrolling\b0 , use this slider. Move the slider to the right for faster scrolling or to the left for slower scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=189\cf0\par
To adjust the force required for both cursor movement and Press-to-Select on the pointing stick, use the \b Touch Sensitivity\b0  slider.\par
\par
To decrease the amount of force required, move the slider toward \b Light Touch\b0 .\par
\par
To increase the amount of force required, move the slider toward \b Heavy Touch\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=190\cf0\par
To keep the pointer inside the active window, select the \b Stop Pointer at Window Borders\b0  check box. Stopping the pointer at the window borders makes it easy to access controls near the edges of a window.\par
\par
With this option selected, the first time you try to move the pointer outside the window, it stops at the edge. If you try a second time, the pointer leaves the window.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=191\cf0\par
To improve pointing accuracy for fine positioning, select an option from the \b Slow Motion Key\b0  drop-down list. This feature allows you to reduce the speed of the pointer.\par
\par
To use slow motion, press and hold the selected key while moving the pointer.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=192\cf0\par
To automatically move the pointer to the default setting when a dialog box appears on your screen, select this feature.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=193\cf0\par
To force purely horizontal or vertical motion of the pointer on the screen, select an option from the \b Constrained Pointer Key\b0  drop-down list.\par
\par
To use constrained pointer motion, press and hold the specified key while moving the pointer. When the key is pressed, the initial direction of your pointer motion determines the direction of movement for as long as your finger remains on the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=194\cf0\par
To receive feedback about your pointing actions, select the \b Animated\b0  check box. The animated icon will display in the Windows taskbar. Double-click on this icon to access the \b Mouse Properties\b0  window.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=195\cf0\par
To have very smooth pointer motion, select the \b Fast\b0  pointer refresh rate option.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=196\cf0\par
If your pointer appears unsteady or flickers on the screen, try selecting the \b Slow\b0  pointer refresh rate.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=197\cf0\par
This is the version number of your Synaptics TouchPad driver.\par
\par
