\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=199\cf0\par
\b Dual Mode\b0\par
The Dual Mode feature allows you to switch from Cursor Mode to Button Mode. In Button Mode, the virtual buttons on the TouchPad surface provide convenient shortcuts to frequently used applications and there is a volume controller for quick and easy access to increasing or decreasing sound volume.\par
\par
To enable Dual Mode, select the \b Switch to Button Mode by tapping in the upper-right corner\b0  checkbox.\par
\par
To use Button Mode:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
\pard{\pntext\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180 Tap on the \b Mode Switch\b0  button. This button is in the upper right corner of the TouchPad above the volume controller.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}Tap on the virtual button for the application you wish to launch. The TouchPad automatically returns to Cursor Mode after the application opens.\par
\pard\tx180\par
Note: You can use Button Mode when an external pointing device, such as a mouse, is\~connected if you select the \b Always in Button Mode when an external device is plugged in\b0  checkbox.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=200\cf0\par
\b Button Mode Options\b0\par
There are six virtual buttons which can be configured to meet your preferences. The upper three virtual buttons have default settings and the others are un-configured. The default settings for the configured virtual buttons are listed below:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
\pard{\pntext\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-180\li180\tx180 A tap on the upper left virtual button opens the assigned email application.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper middle virtual button opens the ConfigFree application. Refer\~to the Toshiba online Help for additional information.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper right virtual button sends the item in the active window to\~the printer.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=201\cf0\par
\b Button Mode Options\b0\par
There are six virtual buttons which can be configured to meet your preferences. Four of\~the virtual buttons have default settings and the others are un-configured. The default settings for the configured virtual buttons are listed below:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
\pard{\pntext\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-180\li180\tx180 A tap on the upper left virtual button opens the assigned email application.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper middle virtual button opens the assigned Web browser.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper right virtual button opens the BeatJam application. Refer\~to the Toshiba online Help for additional information.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the lower left virtual button opens the Toshiba online Help.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=202\cf0\par
The \b Button Check\b0  feature guards against operating the Buttons with accidental contact. Button Check allows the Buttons to recognize when your palm is resting on them or brushing their surface while you are typing. This will help to prevent unwanted button activation.\par
\par
If the button exhibits undesired activation, increase the Button Check setting by moving the slider to the right toward \b Maximum\b0 . If the button misses intended activations, decrease the Button Check setting by moving the slider to the left toward \b Minimum\b0 . You are more likely to experience missed buttons if the slider is all the way to the right.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=203\cf0\par
To configure the action performed when you touch a button, select the button from the menu tree. For example, you could define Button 1 to "go to a web page in your default browser."  Each time you touch Button 1, your web browser would automatically open and load the specified page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=204\cf0\par
The \b Sensitivity\b0  settings allow you to personalize the way the device responds to your touch. To adjust the Sensitivity settings, select an item from the \b Sensitivity\b0  menu tree.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=205\cf0\par
The \b Touch Sensitivity\b0  slider allows you to adjust how much finger pressure must be applied before the device responds.\par
\par
If the response of the device is erratic, or you work in an area with high humidity, decrease sensitivity by moving the slider to the right toward \b Heavy Touch\b0 .\par
\par
If you have to push too hard to operate the device, increase sensitivity by moving the slider to the left toward \b Light Touch\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=206\cf0\par
Select an action from the list above to assign it to the selected button. The selected action will occur every time you touch the button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=207\cf0\par
The \b Enable sounds\b0  check box enables playing a user configurable sound when a button is touched.\par
When \b Enable sounds\b0  is not selected, the sounds are disabled and touching the buttons does not play any sound.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=208\cf0\par
The \b Enable illumination\b0  check box enables illuminating the buttons when they are inactive and active.\par
When the \b Enable illumination\b0  check box is not checked, illumination is disabled and touching the buttons does not change the brightness of the buttons.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=209\cf0\par
The \b Inactive Brightness\b0  slider allows you to adjust the brightness level of the buttons when they are not touched.\par
If the brightness level of the buttons is too high when they are not touched then move the slider towards the \b Minimum\b0  setting.\par
If the brightness level of the buttons is too low when they are not touched then move the slider towards the \b Maximum\b0  setting.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=210\cf0\par
The \b Active Brightness\b0  slider allows you to adjust the brightness level of the buttons when they are touched.\par
If the brightness level of the buttons is too high when they are touched then move the slider towards the \b Minimum\b0  setting.\par
If the brightness level of the buttons is too low when they are touched then move the slider towards the \b Maximum\b0  setting.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=211\cf0\par
\b Button Mode Options\b0\par
There are six virtual buttons which can be configured to meet your preferences. The upper three virtual buttons have default settings and the others are un-configured. The default settings for the configured virtual buttons are listed below:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
\pard{\pntext\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-180\li180\tx180 A tap on the upper left virtual button opens the assigned email application.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper middle virtual button opens the ConfigFree application. Refer\~to the Toshiba online Help for additional information.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper right virtual button opens Windows Photo Gallery.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=212\cf0\par
To configure the action performed when you slide your finger over a slider, select the slider from the menu tree. For example, you could define Slider 1 to "Adjust the Volume on your system."  Each time you slide your finger over Slider 1, the volume would increase or decrease.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=213\cf0\par
Select an action from the above set of actions to assign to the selected slider. The selected action will occur every time you slide your finger up or down on the slider.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=214\cf0\par
\b Adjust Pointer Motion Speed\b0\par
This slider controls the speed of the pointer. You can choose the pointer speed by adjusting the slider between \b Slow\b0  and \b Fast\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=215\cf0\par
\b Button Mode Options\b0\par
There are six virtual buttons which can be configured to meet your preferences. The upper three virtual buttons have default settings and the others are un-configured. The default settings for the configured virtual buttons are listed below:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
\pard{\pntext\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-180\li180\tx180 A tap on the upper left virtual button opens the assigned email application.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper middle virtual button opens the ConfigFree application. Refer\~to the Toshiba online Help for additional information.\par
{\pntext\'B7\tab}A tap on the upper right virtual button opens My Pictures folder.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=216\cf0\par
Control Bar\par
\par
The control bar allows you to configure buttons to launch your favorite applications. You can personalize the look-and-feel of the control bar through the illumination, fadeout timing, and touch sensitivity settings. The control bar also provides easy access to volume control for software sound effects.\par
\par
Select the \b Enable\b0  option to activate the control bar for use. If you do not wish to use the control bar, you can select the \b Disable\b0  option.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=217\cf0\par
LED Illumination\par
\par
You can use the \b LED Illumination\b0  slider to adjust the brightness of the LEDs in the control bar.\par
\par
To increase brightness, move the slider to the right toward \b Bright\b0 .\par
\par
To decrease brightness, move the slider to the left toward \b Dim\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=218\cf0\par
Fadeout Speed\par
\par
You can use the \b Fadeout Speed\b0  slider to set how quickly the LEDs in the control bar fade out after you have touched the control bar and lifted your finger up off of it.\par
\par
To fade out slowly, move the slider to the right toward \b Slow\b0 .\par
\par
To fade out quickly, move the slider to the left toward \b Quick\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=219\cf0\par
Touch Sensitivity\par
\par
The \b Touch Sensitivity\b0  slider allows you to adjust how much finger pressure must be applied before the control bar responds to your touch.\par
\par
If the control bar does not respond to your touch or you have to press hard to operate it, increase sensitivity by moving the slider to the left toward \b Light\b0 .\par
\par
If you inadvertently operate the control bar with a light touch, decrease sensitivity by moving the slider to the right toward \b Heavy\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=220\cf0\par
Sound Effects\par
\par
To assign a sound effect to a specific action, enter a sound file or Browse and select a sound file.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=221\cf0\par
Volume\par
\par
The \b Volume\b0  slider provides an easy way to control the volume of the sound effects.\par
\par
To increase the volume, move the slider to the right toward \b Loud\b0 .\par
\par
To decrease the volume, move the slider to the left toward \b Quiet\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=222\cf0\par
Applications\par
\par
To assign a button to launch an application, enter an executable file or Browse and select an application to launch.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=223\cf0\par
The \b TouchPad Disable Zone\b0  feature allows you to enable or disable the TouchPad by touching the corner and holding your finger still for a brief time.\par
If the TouchPad is enabled, touch the corner to disable it. If the TouchPad is disabled, touch the corner to enable it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=224\cf0\par
The \b Minimum Touch Time\b0  slider allows you to change how long you must touch the corner before the TouchPad will be enabled or disabled.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=225\cf0\par
Virtual Scrolling provides an innovative alternative to conventional scrolling methods. Virtual Scrolling operates the scroll bars automatically so there is no need to move the pointer away from your work.\par
\par
Virtual Scrolling works with most scrollable windows such as word processing documents, spreadsheets, and large digital images. It also works with other scrollable items such as file lists and font lists.\par
\par
ChiralMotion scrolling, a unique kind of Virtual Scrolling, allows you to scroll continuously with one, smooth circular motion. Scrolling speed is controlled by the speed of finger motion. To scroll rapidly, make small quick circles. To scroll slowly, make large circles and move your finger slowly. The direction of scrolling can be reversed by changing the direction of motion.\par
\par
To Use Vertical Scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable vertical scrolling\b0  check box.\par
2.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
3.\tab Slide your finger up or down the right edge of the TouchPad in the desired direction of scrolling.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To Use ChiralMotion to Scroll Vertically:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable vertical scrolling\b0  check box and the \b Enable ChiralMotion scrolling\b0  check box.\par
2.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
3.\tab Place your finger on the TouchPad near the center of the right edge.\par
4.\tab Slide your finger up or down in the desired direction of scrolling and then begin making a circular motion. Your finger can drift towards the center of the TouchPad while making a clockwise or counterclockwise motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To Use Horizontal Scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0  check box.\par
2.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
3.\tab Slide your finger left or right along the lower edge of the TouchPad in the desired direction of scrolling.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To Use ChiralMotion to Scroll Horizontally:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0  check box and the \b Enable ChiralMotion scrolling\b0  check box.\par
2.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
3.\tab Place your finger on the TouchPad near the center of the lower edge.\par
4.\tab Slide your finger left or right in the desired direction of scrolling and then begin making a circular motion. Your finger can drift towards the center of the TouchPad while making a clockwise or counterclockwise motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
If Virtual Scrolling does not respond, ensure that the desired \b Enable scrolling\b0  check box or boxes are selected and that the window or item you have clicked on is scrollable. If you still have problems, check your \b Scrolling Region\b0  settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=226\cf0\par
The \b Momentum\'99\b0  feature delivers smooth, fast, trackball-like mouse movement. One quick flick of the finger on the TouchPad surface can move the cursor across the entire computer screen. Momentum reduces the repetitive motion and fatigue associated with moving the cursor long distances, while providing a smooth and natural feel similar to using a trackball. Momentum is ideal for Real Time Strategy (RTS) games, First Person Shooter (FPS) games, and Role Playing Games (RPG).\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=227\cf0\par
The \b Glide distance\b0  slider enables you to adjust how far the cursor travels in response to the flick of a finger on the TouchPad. To stop the cursor, tap once or place your finger down on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
Select the \b Enable bounce off\b0  screen edge check box to keep the cursor within the boundaries of the computer screen. The \b bounce off screen edge\b0  feature makes it easy to access menu options and controls that are near the edges of the screen.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=228\cf0\par
The \b Pinch\b0  gesture delivers zooming functionality in many applications. The \b Pinch\b0  gesture can be used to perform the same function as a scroll wheel on standard Windows applications that support CTRL-SCROLL WHEEL zoom functionality.\par
\par
To perform a pinch gesture, place two fingers on the TouchPad surface. Move them closer together or further apart. To zoom in, place your fingers on the TouchPad surface near to each other and then slide them apart. To zoom out, begin by placing your fingers apart and then moving them closer together.\par
\par
The best zooming experience results when the finger tips of the two fingers are used for the gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=229\cf0\par
\b Two-finger ChiralMotion\b0  scrolling provides an innovative alternative to conventional scrolling methods. You can scroll upwards or downwards by simply placing two fingers on the TouchPad and making a circular motion. There is no need to keep both fingers on the TouchPad after scrolling has begun. Scrolling will continue for as long as one finger remains on the TouchPad surface making a circular motion.\par
\par
Finger speed controls the speed of scrolling. To scroll rapidly, make small circles and move your finger quickly. To scroll slowly, make large circles and move your finger slowly. The direction of scrolling can be reversed by changing the direction of motion.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=230\cf0\par
An \b Advanced gesture\b0  is an action that the user makes on a Synaptics TouchPad sensor which is recognized by the host device to perform a specified function.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=231\cf0\par
A \b Flick\b0  gesture is placing two fingers on the TouchPad surface, slightly separated, and sliding them across the TouchPad in a straight, continuous motion, and then simultaneously lifting both fingers off the TouchPad surface. The action initiated by the Flick gesture is application specific; for example, in Internet Explorer a left flick is "Browse Back."\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=232\cf0\par
The \b ChiralRotate\b0  gesture rotates the selected object in 90 degree increments, in either clockwise or counter-clockwise directions. The ChiralRotate gesture works only in supported applications.\par
\par
To initiate ChiralRotate, place one finger in the left vertical zone on the TouchPad and move your finger in a circular motion. In supported applications, the selected object will be rotated in 90 degree increments.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=233\cf0\par
Select the \b Enable Pinch\b0  check box to enable this feature.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=234\cf0\par
Select the \b Enable ChiralRotate\b0  check box to enable this feature.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=235\cf0\par
Select the \b Enable Flick\b0  check box to enable this feature.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=236\cf0\par
To see a demonstration of how to use the Pinch gesture, select the \b Show Video\b0  button, under the \b Enable Pinch\b0  check box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=237\cf0\par
To see a demonstration of how to use the ChiralRotate gesture, select the \b Show Video\b0  button, under the \b Enable ChiralRotate\b0  check box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=238\cf0\par
To see a demonstration of how to use the Flick gesture, select the \b Show Video\b0  button, under the \b Enable Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=239\cf0\par
To see a demonstration of how to use the Momentum gesture, select the \b Show Video\b0  button, under the \b Enable Momentum\b0  check box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=240\cf0\par
\par
Virtual Scrolling provides an innovative alternative to conventional scrolling methods. Virtual Scrolling operates the scroll bars automatically so there is no need to move the pointer away from your work.\par
\par
Virtual Scrolling works with most scrollable windows such as word processing documents, spreadsheets, and large digital images. It also works with other scrollable items such as file lists and font lists.\par
\par
Linear scrolling, one of Virtual Scrolling, allows you to do conventional scrolling.\par
\par
