3.\tab Select the \b Long Distance Scrolling\b0 .\par
4.\tab Click the \b Linear scrolling\b0  radio button.\par
5.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
6.\tab Slide your finger up or down the right edge of the TouchPad in the desired direction of scrolling.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To Use ChiralMotion to Scroll Vertically:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable vertical scrolling\b0  check box.\par
2.\tab Click the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  icon or double click the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0 .\par
3.\tab Select the \b Long Distance Scrolling\b0 .\par
4.\tab Click the \b ChiralMotion scrolling\b0  radio button.\par
5.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
6.\tab Place your finger on the TouchPad near the center of the right edge.\par
7.\tab Slide your finger up or down in the desired direction of scrolling and then begin making a circular motion. Your finger can drift towards the center of the TouchPad while making a clockwise or counterclockwise motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To Use Linear Horizontal Scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0  check box.\par
2.\tab Click the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  icon or double click the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0 .\par
3.\tab Select the \b Long Distance Scrolling\b0 .\par
4.\tab Click the \b Linear scrolling\b0  radio button.\par
5.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
6.\tab Slide your finger left or right along the lower edge of the TouchPad in the desired direction of scrolling.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To Use ChiralMotion to Scroll Horizontally:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the \b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0  check box.\par
2.\tab Click the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0  icon or double click the \b Virtual Scrolling\b0 .\par
3.\tab Select the \b Long Distance Scrolling\b0 .\par
4.\tab Click the \b ChiralMotion scrolling\b0  radio button.\par
5.\tab Click on the window or item you wish to scroll.\par
6.\tab Place your finger on the TouchPad near the center of the lower edge.\par
7.\tab Slide your finger left or right in the desired direction of scrolling and then begin making a circular motion. Your finger can drift towards the center of the TouchPad while making a clockwise or counterclockwise motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
If Virtual Scrolling does not respond, ensure that the desired \b Enable scrolling\b0  check box or boxes are selected and that the window or item you have clicked on is scrollable. If you still have problems, check your \b Scrolling Region\b0  settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=241\cf0\par
To see a demonstration of how to use Virtual Scrolling, select the \b Show Video\b0  button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=242\cf0\par
To see a demonstration of how to use the \b Flick\b0  gesture, select the \b Show Video\b0  button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=243\cf0\par
To use vertical flick gestures, select the \b Enable Vertical Flick\b0  check box.  Place two fingers, slightly separated, on the TouchPad and slide up or down. When done, lift them to perform an application-specific function.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=244\cf0\par
To use horizontal flick gestures, select the \b Enable Horizontal Flick\b0  check box.  Place two fingers, slightly separated, on the TouchPad and slide right or left. When done, lift them to perform an application-specific function.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=245\cf0\par
Click the \b Show Video\b0  button to see it in action.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=246\cf0\par
This \b Properties\b0  window allows you to personalize your pointing device settings.  Use the menu tree to the left to access the customizable features.  These include single-finger pointing and scrolling options, plus multi-finger gestures to manipulate documents, objects, and applications.\par
\par
\b Using the Properties Window\b0\par
Click on a feature in the menu tree to see a page of descriptions and settings for that feature.  Some features have several pages of settings.  Click on the + (plus sign) next to a feature to see all its related pages.  The settings will appear in the window to the right of the menu tree.  Information about these options and instructions on how to change them will appear here in this information box.  You can use the scroll bar to view the contents of the information box.\par
\par
\b Defaults Button\b0\par
The \b Defaults\b0  button returns all the settings on the current page back to their factory-set values.\par
\par
\b OK Button\b0\par
The \b OK\b0  button saves your changes and closes the Properties window.\par
\par
\b Cancel Button\b0\par
The \b Cancel\b0  button closes the Properties window without saving any changes and reverts to all the previous settings.\par
\par
\b Apply Button\b0\par
The \b Apply\b0  button saves your changes, but keeps the Properties window open so you can make more changes.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=250\cf0\par
The \b Pointing\b0  settings pages enable you to specify how the cursor responds to your pointing and selection actions.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=251\cf0\par
Select the \b Enable Momentum\b0  check box to use this feature.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=252\cf0\par
The \b Constrained Motion\b0  feature allows you to force purely horizontal or vertical motion of the pointer on the screen. This feature is most useful when using a drawing program. You activate Constrained Motion by pressing and holding a specified key while moving the pointer. When the key is pressed, the initial direction of your pointer determines the allowed direction of movement for as long as your finger remains on the TouchPad.\par
\par
To use Constrained Motion:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select the Enable Constrained Motion check box to enable the options in the list box.\par
2.\tab Choose a key from the list box for activating Constrained Motion.\par
3.\tab Press and hold the selected key whenever you want to constrain pointer motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=254\cf0\par
\b Scrolling\b0\par
The scrolling features allow you to operate the scroll bars of the selected window automatically; there is no need to move the pointer away from your work. The scrolling features work with most scrollable windows such as documents, spreadsheets, large digital images, and other scrollable items such as file lists and font lists.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=255\cf0\par
\b Reverse Scrolling\b0\par
The \b Reverse Scrolling Direction\b0  feature allows you to change standard scrolling behavior. Standard scrolling moves the scroll bar in the direction of finger movement. The Reverse Scrolling Direction feature moves the document or object in the direction of finger motion.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable reverse scrolling direction\b0  check box.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=256\cf0\par
The \b Scrolling speed\b0  slider allows you to adjust the speed of scrolling. Drag the slider to the right for faster scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=257\cf0\par
\b ChiralScrolling\b0\par
The ChiralScrolling\'99 feature enables you to scroll continuously with one, smooth, circular motion. Scrolling speed is controlled by finger speed.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable ChiralScrolling\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use ChiralScrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the \i target\i0  zone. To scroll up or down, the target zone is the right side of the TouchPad. To scroll left or right, the target zone is the lower edge of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Move your finger in the direction you want to scroll, and then begin moving your finger in clockwise or counterclockwise circles. Once ChiralScrolling is initiated, your finger can drift anywhere on the TouchPad surface while making a circular motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
NOTE: To change the direction of scrolling, simply reverse the direction of finger motion. You will begin scrolling in the opposite direction.\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
3.\tab To stop scrolling and return to pointing mode, lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=258\cf0\par
\b One-Finger Scrolling\b0\par
You can scroll vertically and horizontally.\par
\par
Click on the window or item you wish to scroll and slide your finger upwards or downwards along the right edge of the TouchPad to scroll vertically.  Slide your finger to the left or right along the lower edge of the TouchPad to scroll horizontally.\par
\par
If scrolling does not respond, ensure that the appropriate check box is selected and that the window or item on which you have clicked is scrollable. If you still have problems, check your \b Scrolling Region\b0  settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=259\cf0\par
The diagram above represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the scrolling regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger's position. If your finger is in an active scrolling region on the TouchPad, the corresponding scrolling area on the diagram will turn a solid color. You can make the scrolling regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the edge of the cross-hatched area.\par
\par
If you have difficulty triggering scrolling, try making the scrolling region larger by moving the edges of the cross-hatched area inward. If you find that scrolling triggers too easily, try making the scrolling region smaller.\par
\par
If the scrolling regions are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that the scrolling feature is enabled.\par
\par
To enable scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select \b Scrolling\b0  in the menu tree to the left.\par
2.\tab Select the desired method of scrolling, and enable vertical or horizontal scrolling.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=260\cf0\par
\b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0\par
Select this check box to enable horizontal scrolling on windows that have horizontal scroll bars. Sliding your finger to the left or right along the lower edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=261\cf0\par
\b Enable vertical scrolling\b0\par
Select this check box to enable vertical scrolling. Sliding your finger up or down along the right edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item vertically.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=262\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Scrolling\b0\par
The Two-Finger Scrolling feature allows you to scroll vertically or horizontally and initiate scrolling from anywhere on the TouchPad surface. Simply place two fingers down on the TouchPad and then slide them in the desired direction. You do not need to be in a scrolling region to scroll.\par
\par
To use Two-Finger Scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place two fingers down on the TouchPad surface. The fingers should be slightly separated.\par
2.\tab Using a straight continuous motion, slide both fingers in the desired direction.\par
3.\tab Lift both fingers up off the TouchPad surface to stop scrolling.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=263\cf0\par
\b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0\par
Select this check box to enable horizontal scrolling on windows that have horizontal scroll bars. Sliding your two fingers to the left or right on the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=264\cf0\par
\b Enable vertical scrolling\b0\par
Select this check box to enable vertical scrolling. Sliding your two fingers up or down on the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item vertically.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=265\cf0\par
The \b Coasting\b0  feature helps you to scroll easily through long documents. With Coasting, scrolling continues after you have lifted your finger up off the TouchPad surface. To use Coasting, move and then lift your finger in one smooth motion. To stop the coasting motion, place your finger back down on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=266\cf0\par
\b Pinch Zoom\b0\par
The Pinch Zoom gesture delivers zooming functionality in many applications. You can use the Pinch Zoom gesture to perform the same functions that a scroll wheel performs on standard Windows applications that support CTRL-SCROLL WHEEL zoom functionality.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Pinch Zoom\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use a Pinch Zoom gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place two fingers down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Slide the fingers further apart to zoom in or slide the fingers closer together to zoom out. For the best zooming experience, use the tips of your fingers.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=267\cf0\par
The \b Rotating\b0  features provide convenient gestures for rotating photos and other objects.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=269\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Press\b0\par
The Three-Finger Press gesture provides a shortcut for launching a specified application or applications.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Three-Finger Press\b0  check box. To customize the applications that launch, click the \b Configure\b0  button.\par
\par
To use the Three-Finger Press feature:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers down on the TouchPad surface. Ensure that there is a small gap between the fingers.\par
2.\tab Hold your fingers still until the application or applications begin to launch.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=270\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Three-Finger-Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Three-Finger-Flick gesture for browsing the Internet or scrolling through photos in a photo viewer window. You can also use the Three-Finger-Flick gesture to enter or exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Three-Finger Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Three-Finger-Flick gesture for navigation:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers, slightly separated, down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a linear, continuous motion in the desired direction, moving from one side of the TouchPad to the opposite side.\par
3.\tab Horizontal finger motion, sliding your fingers to the right or left will page forwards or backwards in a photo viewer or an Internet browser window.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To use the Three-Finger-Flick gesture for slideshow mode, slide your fingers vertically. Slide your fingers upwards to launch slideshow mode. Slide your fingers downwards to exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=271\cf0\par
\b Palm on Pad\b0\par
The Palm-on-Pad gesture enables you to clear your desktop by minimizing all open windows. Repeating this gesture restores the windows.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Palm on Pad\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Palm-on-Pad gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place the \i heel\i0  of your thumb down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Hold your hand still until your desktop windows have been minimized or restored.\par
3.\tab Lift your hand up off the TouchPad.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=272\cf0\par
\b Practice Scrolling\b0\par
Click the \b Practice\b0  button to run the scrolling tutorial. A simple maze will help you learn how to use scrolling.\par
\par
Survey the maze before you begin to locate the beginning and ending points. You cannot see the entire maze because it is larger than the window that displays it. Scroll to move your view of the maze in the window. Hold down the Ctrl key and use scrolling to resize the maze.\par
\par
A yellow dot in the upper left corner of the maze marks the entrance to the maze. A blue dot on the right side of the maze marks the exit.\par
\par
To practice scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Click on the red square in the yellow dot to begin.\par
2.\tab Use the Arrow keys or tap your TouchPad to navigate through the maze.\par
3.\tab Use the desired scrolling method to move your view of the maze.\par
4.\tab Click the Close button on the scrolling practice window after you reach the blue dot in the lower right corner.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=273\cf0\par
\b HP MediaSmart M-Gesture\b0\par
The HP MediaSmart application can be launched by performing an M-shaped gesture on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable HP MediaSmart M-Gesture\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use this gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Tap two fingers on the TouchPad surface, then immediately place them back down.\par
2.\tab Slide the fingers in a large \i M\i0  shape pattern.\par
3.\tab The HP MediaSmart application will launch.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=274\cf0\par
The \b Pointing Enhancements\b0  features enable you to specify how the cursor responds to your pointing actions.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=275\cf0\par
Application gestures provide shortcuts to frequently used applications or functions. The computer\rquote s response to these gestures can be application-specific.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=276\cf0\par
The \b EdgeMotion\'99\b0  feature is designed to simplify long-distance pointer motions. When your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad, the pointer will continue to move in the same direction until you lift your finger. Select one of the EdgeMotion options.\par
\par
The scrolling features also support EdgeMotion. To use EdgeMotion while scrolling, select the \b Enable EdgeMotion when scrolling\b0  under \b One-Finger Scrolling\b0  or \b Two-Finger Scrolling\b0  under \b Scrolling\b0  in the menu tree.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=277\cf0\par
Select the \b Enable EdgeMotion when scrolling\b0  check box if you want scrolling to continue when your fingers reach the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=278\cf0\par
The \b Sensitivity\b0  settings allow you to personalize the way the TouchPad responds to your touch.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=279\cf0\par
The \b Coasting\b0  feature helps you to scroll easily through long documents. With Coasting, scrolling continues after you have lifted your fingers up off the TouchPad surface. To use Coasting, move and lift your fingers in one smooth motion. To stop the coasting motion, place your fingers back down on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=280\cf0\par
\b Pivot Rotate\b0\par
The Pivot Rotate gesture allows you to rotate objects such as a photo, a Portable Document Format (PDF) file, or other selected object, in 90 degree increments.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Pivot Rotate\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use a Pivot Rotate gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the lower left corner of the TouchPad, and keep it stationary.\par
2.\tab Place another finger down near the middle of the TouchPad.\par
3.\tab Slide the second finger in a circular motion around first finger. Ensure that the space between the two fingers remains the same while making the circular motion. Slide clockwise or counterclockwise to rotate the selected object in the desired direction.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=281\cf0\par
\b Right Corner Pivot\b0\par
To use the Pivot Rotate feature in the lower right corner of the TouchPad, select the \b Right Corner Pivot\b0  check box. Using the right corner of the TouchPad may be more comfortable when performing this gesture with the left hand.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=282\cf0\par
\b ChiralRotate\b0\par
The ChiralRotate\'99 gesture rotates the selected object in 90 degree increments.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the Enable ChiralRotate check box. Note that the ChiralRotate gesture works only with supported applications.\par
\par
To use the ChiralRotate gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the left \i target\i0  zone of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Slide your finger downwards or upwards and begin making a circular motion. To rotate the object in a clockwise direction, move your finger in clockwise circles. To rotate the object in a counterclockwise direction, move your finger in counterclockwise circles.\par
3.\tab Lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface to return to pointing mode.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=283\cf0\par
\b Switch TouchPad Off\b0\par
If you do not wish to use the TouchPad, you can switch it off. Place one finger down on the upper left corner of the TouchPad and hold your finger still for a moment. The TouchPad will be switched off. The TouchPad icon in the task bar will change to indicate that the TouchPad is unavailable for use.\par
\par
To switch the TouchPad back on, touch the upper left corner again. The icon in the taskbar will return to the enabled state.\par
\par
To specify how long the TouchPad will remain off, select the desired option.\par
The \b Switch TouchPad off\b0  option will keep the TouchPad off until you restore it for use by touching the upper left corner.\par
\par
The \b Switch TouchPad off for one session\b0  option will switch the TouchPad off for one logon session. The TouchPad will automatically be restored for use the next time you log on.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=284\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Two-Finger-Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture for browsing the Internet or scrolling through photos in a photo viewer window. You can also use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture to enter or exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Two-Finger Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture for navigation:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place two fingers, slightly separated, down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a linear, continuous motion in the desired direction, moving from one side of the TouchPad to the opposite side.\par
3.\tab Horizontal finger motion, sliding your fingers to the right or left will page forwards or backwards in a photo viewer or an Internet browser window.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture for slideshow mode, slide your fingers vertically. Slide your fingers upwards to launch slideshow mode. Slide your fingers downwards to exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=285\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Two-Finger-Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture for browsing the Internet or scrolling through photos in a photo viewer window.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Two-Finger Horizontal Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture for navigation:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place two fingers, slightly separated, down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers horizontally in a straight, continuous motion in the desired direction, moving from one side of the TouchPad to the opposite side.\par
3.\tab Slide your fingers to the right or left to page forwards or backwards in a photo viewer or an Internet browser window.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=286\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Two-Finger-Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture to enter or exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Two-Finger Vertical Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Two-Finger-Flick gesture for navigation:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place two fingers, slightly separated, down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Slide your fingers vertically in a straight, continuous motion in the desired direction.\par
3.\tab Slide your fingers upwards to launch slideshow mode or downwards to exit slideshow mode.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=287\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Horizontal Flick\b0\par
The Three-Finger Horizontal Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Three-Finger Horizontal Flick gesture for browsing the Internet or scrolling through photos in a photo viewer window.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Three-Finger Horizontal Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Three-Finger Horizontal Flick gesture for navigation:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers, slightly separated, down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers horizontally in a straight, continuous motion in the desired direction, moving from one side of the TouchPad to the opposite side.\par
3.\tab Slide your fingers to the right or left to page forwards or backwards in a photo viewer or an Internet browser window.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=288\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Vertical Flick\b0\par
The Three-Finger Vertical Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Three-Finger Vertical Flick gesture to enter or exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Three-Finger Vertical Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Three-Finger Vertical Flick gesture for navigation:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers, slightly separated, down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Slide your fingers vertically in a straight, continuous motion in the desired direction.\par
3.\tab Slide your fingers upwards to launch slideshow mode or downwards to exit slideshow mode.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=289\cf0\par
\b Circular Scrolling\b0\par
The Circular Scrolling feature enables you to scroll continuously with one, smooth, circular motion. Scrolling speed is controlled by finger speed.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Circular Scrolling\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use Circular Scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the \i target\i0  zone. To scroll up or down, the target zone is along the right edge of the TouchPad. To scroll left or right, the target zone is along the lower edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
2.\tab Slide your finger in the direction you want to scroll, and then begin moving your finger in clockwise or counterclockwise circles. Once Circular Scrolling is initiated, your finger can drift anywhere on the TouchPad surface while making a circular motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
NOTE: To change the direction of scrolling, simply reverse the direction of finger motion. You will begin scrolling in the opposite direction.\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
3.\tab To stop scrolling and return to pointing mode, lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=290\cf0\par
The \b Auto Scrolling\b0  feature helps you to scroll easily through long documents with a single stroke of the finger. Scrolling continues after you have lifted your finger up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
To use Auto Scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Click on the windows or item you wish to scroll.\par
2.\tab Slide one finger, along the scrolling region of the TouchPad, in the direction you want to scroll. To scroll up or down, the scrolling region is the right edge. To scroll left or right, the scrolling region is the lower edge.\par
\pard\tx180\par
If Auto Scrolling does not respond, check your \b Scrolling Region\b0  settings. If you still have problems using Auto Scrolling, ensure that the window or item you have clicked on is scrollable.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=291\cf0\par
The \b Rotate\b0  features provide convenient gestures for rotating photos and other objects.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=292\cf0\par
\b My Favorites gesture\b0\par
The My Favorites gesture provides a shortcut for launching a specified application or applications.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable My Favorites gesture\b0  check box. To customize the applications that launch, click the \b Configure\b0  button.\par
\par
To use this gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers down on the TouchPad surface. Ensure that there is a small gap between the fingers.\par
2.\tab Hold your fingers still until the application or applications begin to launch.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=293\cf0\par
\b Flick\b0\par
The Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Flick gesture for browsing the Internet or scrolling through photos in a photo viewer window. You can also use the Flick gesture to enter or exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Flick\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use the Flick gesture for navigation:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers down on the TouchPad surface. Ensure that there is a small gap between the fingers\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a linear, continuous motion in the desired direction, moving from one side of the TouchPad to the opposite side.\par
3.\tab Horizontal finger motion, sliding your fingers to the right or left will page forwards or backwards in a photo viewer or an Internet browser window.\par
\pard\tx180\par
To use the Flick gesture for slideshow mode:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Slide your fingers upwards to launch slideshow mode.\par
2.\tab Slide your fingers downwards to exit slideshow mode.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=294\cf0\par
\b Home Gesture\b0\par
The Home Gesture enables you to clear your desktop by minimizing all open windows. Repeating this gesture restores the windows.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Home Gesture\b0 check box.\par
\par
To use the Home Gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place the \i heel\i0  of your thumb down on the TouchPad surface.\par
2.\tab Hold your hand still until your desktop windows have been minimized or restored.\par
3.\tab Lift your hand up off the TouchPad.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=295\cf0\par
\b Zoom Zone\b0\par
With the Zoom Zone feature, you can use the left side of the Touchpad to zoom-in on or zoom-out of the item in the active window. The Zoom Zone feature performs the same zooming functionality as a scroll wheel on standard Windows applications that support CTRL-SCROLL WHEEL.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Zoom Zone\b0  check box.\par
\par
\b To Zoom In:\b0\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place a finger on the Touchpad near the lower left corner.\par
2.\tab Slide the finger upwards toward the upper left corner.\par
3.\tab Repeat until the desired level of magnification is reached.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\b To Zoom Out:\b0\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place a finger on the Touchpad near the upper left corner.\par
2.\tab Slide the finger downwards toward the lower left corner.\par
3.\tab Repeat until the desired level of magnification is reached.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=296\cf0\par
The \b TouchCheck\b0  feature guards against operating the TouchPad with accidental contact. TouchCheck allows the TouchPad to recognize when your palm is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing. This will help to prevent unwanted pointer movement or clicks.\par
\par
If the TouchPad exhibits undesired pointer movement or clicks, increase the TouchCheck setting by moving the slider to the right toward \b Maximum\b0 . If the TouchPad misses intended motions or taps, decrease the TouchCheck setting by moving the slider to the left toward \b Minimum\b0 . You are more likely to experience missed motions or taps if the slider is in the red zone.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=297\cf0\par
\b Rotate\b0\par
The Rotate gesture allows you to rotate objects such as a photo, a Portable Document Format (PDF) file, or other selected object, in 90 degree increments.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Rotate\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use a Rotate gesture:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the lower left corner of the TouchPad, and keep it stationary.\par
2.\tab Place another finger down near the middle of the TouchPad.\par
3.\tab Slide the second finger in a circular motion around first finger. Ensure that the space between the two fingers remains the same while making the circular motion. Slide clockwise or counterclockwise to rotate the selected object in the desired direction.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=298\cf0\par
\b Right Corner Rotate\b0\par
To use the Rotate feature in the lower right corner of the TouchPad, select the \b Right Corner Rotate\b0  check box. Using the right corner of the TouchPad may be more comfortable when performing this gesture with the left hand.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=299\cf0\par
\b Zoom Rate\b0\par
The Zoom Rate slider controls how quickly the window zooms in response to finger motion in the zooming area.\par
\par
To zoom slower, move the slider toward \b Slow\b0 .\par
To zoom faster, move the slider toward \b Fast\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=300\cf0\par
ClickZones perform the same function as physical buttons. There are two ClickZones along the lower edge of the ClickPad. Pressing a ClickZone is like clicking a left or right button. To use ClickZones, select the \b Enable ClickZones \b0 option.\par
\par
If you do not enable ClickZones, you must enable \b Tapping \b0 or use an external mouse to perform a primary or secondary button click.\par
\par
The left ClickZone performs a primary click. The right ClickZone performs a secondary click. To change these settings, select \b Left Click \b0 or \b Right Click \b0 and configure it as desired.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=301\cf0\par
ClickZones perform the same function as physical buttons. There are three ClickZones along the lower edge of the ClickPad: left, middle, and right. Pressing a ClickZone performs the same function as clicking a button. To use ClickZones, select the \b Enable ClickZones \b0 option.\par
\par
If you do not enable ClickZones, you must enable \b Tapping \b0 or use an external mouse to perform a primary or secondary button click.\par
\par
The left ClickZone performs a primary click. The right ClickZone performs a secondary click. The middle ClickZone performs the same function as a middle button. To change these settings, select \b Left Click \b0, \b Middle Click \b0, or \b Right Click \b0 and configure it as desired.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=302\cf0\par
You can select an action from the list to assign it to the left ClickZone.\par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear.\par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=303\cf0\par
You can select an action from the list to assign it to the middle ClickZone.\par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear.\par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=304\cf0\par
You can select an action from the list to assign it to the right ClickZone.\par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear.\par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=305\cf0\par
ClickZones perform the same function as physical buttons. There are two ClickZones along the lower edge of the ClickPad. Pressing a ClickZone is like clicking a left or right button. To use ClickZones, select the \b Enable ClickZones \b0 option.\par
\par
If you do not enable ClickZones, you can enable the physical buttons to perform a primary or secondary click. If you do not enable ClickZones or the physical buttons, you must enable \b Tap Zones \b0 (under the \b Tapping \b0 menu tree) or use an external mouse to perform a primary or secondary click.\par
\par
The left ClickZone and physical button perform a primary click. The right ClickZone and physical button perform a secondary click. To change these settings, select \b Left Click \b0 or \b Right Click \b0 and configure it as desired.\par
\par
Click the \b Show Video \b0 button to see how to use ClickZones.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=306\cf0\par
ClickZones perform the same function as physical buttons. There are three ClickZones along the lower edge of the ClickPad: left, middle, and right. Pressing a ClickZone performs the same function as clicking a left, middle, or right button. To use ClickZones, select the \b Enable ClickZones \b0 option.\par
\par
If you do not enable ClickZones, you can enable physical buttons to perform a primary or secondary click. If you do not enable ClickZones or physical buttons, you must enable \b Tap Zones \b0(under the \b Tapping \b0 menu tree) or use an external mouse to perform a primary or secondary button click.\par
\par
The left ClickZone and button perform a primary click. The right ClickZone and button perform a secondary click. To change these settings, select \b Left Click \b0 or \b Right Click \b0 and configure it as desired.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=307\cf0\par
You can select an action from the list to assign it to the left ClickZone. The option that you select for the left ClickZone is automatically assigned to the left button. For example, if you want to swap the action for the left and right ClickZones, you can select \b Secondary Click\b0. Be sure to change the action for the right ClickZone to make it a \b Primary Click \b0 to complete the change.\par
\par
Many actions do not require additional configuration. If more information is required, the Configure button will become enabled or a configuration dialog box will appear. Click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
Click the \b Show Video \b0 button to see a left ClickZone in use.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=308\cf0\par
You can select an action from the list to assign it to the middle ClickZone. The option that you select for the middle ClickZone is automatically assigned to the middle physical button (if there is a middle physical button). For example, if you select \b Jump to the Start Button\b0, then a middle click will cause the pointer to jump to the \b Start \b0 button in the Windows task bar and automatically open the \b Start Menu\b0.\par
\par
Many actions do not require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a configuration dialog box will appear. Click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
Click the Show Video button to see a middle ClickZone in use.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=309\cf0\par
You can select an action from the list to assign it to the right ClickZone. The option that you select for the right ClickZone is automatically assigned to the right physical button. For example, if you want to swap the action for the left and right ClickZones, you can select \b Primary Click\b0. Be sure to change the action for the right ClickZone to make it a \b Secondary Click \b0 to complete the change.\par
\par
Many actions do not require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a configuration dialog box will appear. Click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
Click the \b Show Video \b0 button to see a right ClickZone in use.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=310\cf0\par
The ClickButtons feature transforms the entire ClickPad surface into one big “virtual” button. You can press anywhere on the ClickPad to perform a primary, secondary, or middle button click. Select the \b Enable ClickButtons \b0 option to use this feature.\par
\par
Pressing one finger down performs a primary click. Pressing two fingers down performs a secondary click. Pressing three fingers down performs a middle button click. To change these settings, select \b One-Finger Click\b0, \b Two-Finger Click\b0, or \b Three-Finger Click \b0 and configure it as desired.\par
\par
If you do not enable ClickButtons, you must enable \b Tapping \b0 or use an external mouse to perform a primary, secondary, or middle button click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=311\cf0\par
To perform a one-finger click, press one finger down on the ClickPad surface until you feel it descend. By default, a one-finger click is equivalent to clicking a primary button. \par
\par
If you wish to change the default setting, select an option from the list. \par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear. \par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=312\cf0\par
To perform a two-finger click, press two fingers down on the ClickPad surface until you feel it descend. By default, a two-finger click is equivalent to clicking a secondary button. \par
\par
If you wish to change the default setting, select an option from the list. \par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear. \par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=313\cf0\par
To perform a three-finger click, press three fingers down on the ClickPad surface until you feel it descend. By default, a three-finger click is equivalent to clicking a middle button. \par
\par
If you wish to change the default setting, select an option from the list. \par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear. \par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings. \par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=314\cf0\par
The ClickButtons feature transforms the entire ClickPad surface into one big “virtual” button. You can press anywhere to perform a primary, secondary, or middle button click. Select the \b Enable ClickButtons \b0 option to use this feature.\par
\par
Pressing one finger down performs a primary click. Pressing two fingers down performs a secondary click. Pressing three fingers down performs a middle button click. To change these settings, select \b One-Finger Click\b0, \b Two-Finger Click\b0, or \b Three-Finger Click \b0 and configure it as desired.\par
\par
If you do not enable ClickButtons, you can enable the physical buttons to perform a primary or secondary click. If you do not enable ClickButtons or the physical buttons, you must enable \b Tap Zones \b0 (under the \b Tapping \b0 menu tree) or use an external mouse to perform a primary, secondary, or middle button click.\par
\par
To modify your button settings, select \b Physical Buttons \b0 in the menu tree and then configure the \b Left Click \b0 and the \b Right Click \b0 as desired.\par
\par
Click the \b Show Video \b0 button to see how to use ClickButtons.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=315\cf0\par
\b TwistRotate \b0\par
The TwistRotate gesture enables you to rotate items such as photos, Portable Document Format (PDF) files, or other items. To enable this feature, select the \b Enable TwistRotate \b0 check box.\par
\par
\b To perform a TwistRotate gesture: \b0\par
1.\tab Select the object you wish to rotate.\par
2.\tab Place two fingers on the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
3.\tab Slide your fingers in a clockwise or counterclockwise direction. The motion is similar to turning a knob.\par
4.\tab Repeat the rotating motion as needed.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=316\cf0\par
\b Four-Finger Flick\b0\par
Vertical and horizontal four-finger flicks provide convenient shortcuts for switching applications and minimizing or restoring your desktop.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=317\cf0\par
\b Enable Vertical Four-Finger Flick\b0\par
When this option is enabled, a downward four-finger flick minimizes or restores the windows on your desktop. An upward four-finger flick opens the application switcher.\par
\par
To minimize the windows on your desktop:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place four fingers near the upper edge of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion towards the lower edge.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab Repeat the gesture to restore the windows on your desktop.\par
\par
To open the Aero 3D application switcher:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place four fingers near the lower edge of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion towards the upper edge.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab The Aero 3D application switcher will open.\par
5.\tab To page through the applications, slide one finger horizontally.\par
6.\tab When the desired application is highlighted, tap on the TouchPad to switch to it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=318\cf0\par
The Click-and-Drag feature allows you to use ClickZones to move things.\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Use your cursor to highlight the item you want to move.\par
2.\tab Press down on the ClickZone that performs a primary click. By default, this is the left ClickZone.\par
3.\tab Keep the ClickZone down while using a second finger to drag the selected item to the desired location.\par
4.\tab Lift your finger off the ClickZone when you want to release the item being dragged.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
If you have difficulty performing a Click-and-Drag gesture, ensure that your ClickZones are enabled and ensure that you are pressing on the ClickZone that is configured to perform a primary click.\par
\par
Select \b Buttons and ClickZones \b0 in the menu tree to check your ClickZone settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=319\cf0\par
You can click-and-drag with one finger to move a selected item. You can click-and-drag with two fingers to perform the equivalent of a right-physical-button click-and-drag.\par
\par
\b To perform a One-Finger Click-and-Drag Gesture:\b0\par
1.\tab Use your cursor to highlight the item you want to move.\par
2.\tab Press one finger down on the ClickPad.\par
3.\tab Keep the ClickPad pressed down while dragging the selected item to the desired location.\par
4.\tab Lift your finger off the ClickPad when you want to release the item.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\b To perform a Two-Finger Click-and-Drag Gesture:\b0\par
1.\tab Use your cursor to highlight the item you want to move. \par
2.\tab Press two-fingers down on the ClickPad.\par
3.\tab Keep the ClickPad pressed down while dragging the selected item to the desired location.\par
4.\tab Lift your fingers off the ClickPad when you want to release the item.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=320\cf0\par
The \b Touch Sensitivity\b0  slider allows you to adjust how much finger pressure must be applied before the Scroll Pad responds.\par
\par
If your Scroll Pad responds erratically, or you work in an area with high humidity, decrease sensitivity by moving the slider to the right toward \b Heavy Touch\b0 .\par
\par
If you have to press too hard to operate the Scroll Pad, increase sensitivity by moving the slider to the left toward \b Light Touch\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=321\cf0\par
The \b Sensitivity\b0  settings allow you to personalize the way the Scroll Pad responds to your touch. To adjust the Sensitivity settings, select an item from the \b Sensitivity\b0  menu tree.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=322\cf0\par
The \b PalmCheck\b0\'99 feature guards against operating the Scroll Pad with accidental contact. PalmCheck allows the Scroll Pad to recognize when your palm is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing. This will help to prevent unwanted scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=323\cf0\par
The \b Enable button\b0  check box enables the button for this device. To configure the action performed when you press the button, select the name of the button from the menu tree.\par
\par
When \b Enable button\b0  is not selected, the button is disabled and pressing it has no effect.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=324\cf0\par
Select an action from the list above to assign it to the selected button. The selected action will occur every time you press the button.\par
\par
Note that the button must be enabled to assign an action to it. To enable the button, perform the following steps:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select \b Button\b0  in the menu tree to the left.\par
2.\tab Select the \b Enable button\b0  check box.\par
3.\tab Select the button from the \b Button\b0  menu tree to return to this page.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=325\cf0\par
These options allow you to specify which window or item is scrolled when you use Scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=326\cf0\par
Some applications allow you to specify the degree of rotation. The option you select in the \b Enable Degree of Rotation \b0 field will be applied if the application supports it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=327\cf0\par
Click the \b Show Video \b0 button to see how to perform this gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=328\cf0\par
With the Enable edge tap filtering option selected, incidental finger tapping along the edges of the TouchPad will be ignored.\par
\par
The edge region is denoted by the cross-hatched area shown above. You can adjust the size of the edge region by dragging the black resize handles. Please note that pointer motion, area selection, scrolling region, and gesture input are not affected by this setting.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=329\cf0\par
The Starting zone feature helps to minimize unintentional pointer motion and area selection.\par
\par
With this option selected, your finger must initially contact the TouchPad in the Starting zone for pointer motion or area selection to occur. If pointer motion or area selection is attempted with initial contact outside of the Starting zone, the contact will be ignored. As long as pointer motion or area selection begins from within the Starting zone, areas of the TouchPad that are outside of the Starting zone will work normally until the Starting zone is reset. By adjusting the slider above, you can specify how soon the Starting zone will reset when no contact occurs on the pad. Tapping, scrolling regions, and gestures are not affected by the Starting zone option.\par
\par
For best results, enable the PalmCheck feature in addition to this feature.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=330\cf0\par
The \b Touch Sensitivity\b0  slider allows you to adjust how much finger pressure must be applied before the Scroll Sensor responds.\par
\par
If your Scroll Sensor responds erratically, or you work in an area with high humidity, decrease sensitivity by moving the slider to the right toward \b Heavy Touch\b0 .\par
\par
If you have to press too hard to operate the Scroll Sensor, increase sensitivity by moving the slider to the left toward \b Light Touch\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=331\cf0\par
The \b Sensitivity\b0  settings allow you to personalize the way the Scroll Sensor responds to your touch. To adjust the Sensitivity settings, select an item from the \b Sensitivity\b0  menu tree.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=332\cf0\par
When Two-Finger Double Tap is enabled, you can simultaneously tap two fingers twice on the TouchPad to launch the application of your choice. (Ensure there is a small gap between your fingers.) Click Configure to select which application you want this gesture to launch.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=333\cf0\par
The Starting zone is denoted by the cross-hatched area shown above. You can adjust the size of the Starting zone by dragging the black resize handles. \par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=334\cf0\par
\b What is a ClickPad?\b0\par
A Synaptics ClickPad™ is similar to a Synaptics TouchPad™. A ClickPad can be used for scrolling, pointing, and selection, just like a TouchPad.\par
\par
A ClickPad, however, descends when pressed. Pressing down on a ClickPad feels similar to pressing a physical button. This makes the ClickPad a convenient alternative to physical buttons or a mouse.\par
\par
Your ClickPad has one or both of the following features:\par
\par
• ClickButtons\par
• ClickZones\par
\par
Select the feature in the menu tree to the left to learn more about it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=335\cf0\par
\b Enable Horizontal Four-Finger Flick\b0\par
With this option enabled, a horizontal four-finger flick to the right opens the application switcher.\par
A horizontal four-finger flick to the left switches to the previously used application.\par
\par
To open the application switcher:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place four fingers near the left side of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion to the right.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab The application switcher will open.\par
5.\tab To page through the applications, slide one finger horizontally.\par
6.\tab When the desired application is highlighted, tap on the TouchPad to switch to it.\par
\par
To switch to the previously used application:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place four fingers near the right side of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion to the left.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=336\cf0\par
\b Magnify Mode\b0\par
With the magnifier option enabled, the pointer can turn into a magnifying glass and used to magnify any part of the screen. Input the magnifier\rquote s size (between 100 and 400 pixels in each direction), and the default zoom level.\par
\par
\b To Enter and Use Magnify Mode:\b0\par
Place one finger on the TouchPad and double tap with a second finger. A rectangle (magnifier) will display.  \par
\par
While in magnify mode, move the magnifier as you would move a pointer to change position. You can use the pinch zoom gesture to increase or decrease magnification level.  \par
\par
\b To Exit Magnify Mode:\b0\par
Click the left mouse button, tap the TouchPad, or press Esc on the keyboard to return to normal pointer mode.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=337\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Single Tap\b0\par
By enabling the two finger single tap, you can configure the TouchPad to act as a button when it is tapped with two fingers. Select anction from the list to assign it to two finger single tap and configure if required.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=338\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Vertical Strokes\b0\par
The Three-Finger Vertical Stroke provides shortcuts to My Computer and the Task Switcher. To enable Three-Finger stroke up, click the \ldblquote My Computer\rdblquote  checkbox. To Enable Three-Finger Stroke Down, click the \ldblquote Switch among windows\rdblquote  checkbox.\par
\par
\b Three-Finger Stroke Up:\b0\par
1.\tab Place three fingers (slightly apart) at the lower edge of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Slide your fingers toward the top edge of the TouchPad.\par
3.\tab The My Computer window will open.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\b Three-Finger Stroke Down:\b0\par
1.\tab Place three fingers (slightly apart) near the top edge of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Slide your fingers down to the lower edge of the TouchPad.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab The Task Switcher will launch.\par
5.\tab Use Three-Finger horizontal strokes to the left or right to switch applications.\par
6.\tab When the desired application is highlighted, lift all your fingers off the TouchPad to switch to it.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=339\cf0\par
The \b EdgeMotion™ \b0 feature is designed to simplify long-distance pointer motions. When your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad, the pointer will continue to move in the same direction until you lift your finger. \par
\par
To enable EdgeMotion, ensure that tapping is enabled, and then select the desired EdgeMotion options. \par
\par
The scrolling features also support EdgeMotion. To use EdgeMotion while scrolling, select the desired scrolling options.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=340\cf0\par
The \b TouchPad Disable Zone\b0  feature allows you to enable or disable the TouchPad by double tapping on the corner zone of the TouchPad.\par
If the TouchPad is enabled, double tap on the corner to disable it. If the TouchPad is disabled, double tap on the corner to enable it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=341\cf0\par
Checking this box turns on the double tap to enable this feature. Unchecking this box turns off the double tap to disable it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=342\cf0\par
Select this check box to enable vertical and horizontal scrolling.\par
Slide your finger up or down on the Touchpad to move the selected item or window vertically.\par
Slide your finger left or right on the Touchpad to move the selected item or window horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=343\cf0\par
Select this check box to enable vertical and horizontal scrolling.\par
Slide your two fingers up or down on the Touchpad to move the selected item or window vertically.\par
Slide your two fingers left or right on the Touchpad to move the selected item or window horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=344\cf0\par
When Two-Finger Double Tap is enabled, you can simultaneously tap two fingers twice on the TouchPad to launch the application of your choice. (Ensure there is a small gap between your fingers.) Click Configure to select which application you want this gesture to launch.\par
\par
To enable Two-Finger Double Tap:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Ensure that \b Enable Tapping\b0  is selected on the \b Tapping\b0  page.\par
2.\tab Select the \b Enable Two-Finger Double Tap\b0  check box above.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=345\cf0\par
\b Switch TouchPad Off\b0\par
If you do not wish to use the TouchPad, you can switch it off. Double tap the upper left corner of the TouchPad. The TouchPad icon in the task bar will change to indicate that the TouchPad is unavailable for use.\par
To switch the TouchPad back on, double tap the upper left corner again. The icon in the taskbar will return to the enabled state.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=346\cf0\par
The \b Switch TouchPad off\b0  option will keep the TouchPad off until you restore it for use by double tapping the upper left corner.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=347\cf0\par
The \b Switch TouchPad off for one session\b0  option will switch the TouchPad off for the rest of the current session. The TouchPad will automatically be restored for use the next time you log on.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=348\cf0\par
\b Sensitivity\b0\par
The Sensitivity settings allow you to personalize the way the Scroll Sensor responds to your touch.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=349\cf0\par
The scrolling features also support EdgeMotion. To use EdgeMotion while scrolling, select the \b Enable EdgeMotion when scrolling\b0  under \b One-Finger Scrolling\b0  or \b Two-Finger Scrolling\b0  under \b Scrolling\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=350\cf0\par
When enabled, this filter helps to predict when you are intentionally using the one-finger scrolling region. If you notice you accidentally trigger scrolling, enable this filter.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=351\cf0\par
When enabled, this filter helps to predict when you are intentionally using Pinch Zoom, Two-Finger Scroll, Rotate, and Application Gestures.\par
\par
The area of the pad that will allow gesture use is denoted by the cross-hatched area shown above. You can adjust the size of this area by dragging the black resize handles.\par
\par
If initial contact occurs outside of cross-hatched area, the gesture will be ignored. If you accidentally trigger Pinch Zoom, Two-Finger Scrolling, or Rotate, enable this filter.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=352\cf0\par
\b MultiFinger Gestures\b0\par
MultiFinger Gestures provide shortcuts to frequently used applications or functions. The computer's response to these gestures can be application-specific. Click on the help for the individual gestures to find out what they do.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=353\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Scrolling\b0\par
The Two-Finger Scrolling feature allows you to scroll vertically or horizontally from anywhere on the TouchPad surface. Place two fingers down on the TouchPad and then slide them in the desired direction. You do not need to be in a scrolling region to scroll.\par
\par
Watch the video to learn how to use Two-Finger Scrolling. Make sure the fingers are slightly apart from each other. Lift both fingers off the TouchPad to stop scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=354\cf0\par
\b Enable vertical scrolling\b0\par
Select this checkbox to enable vertical scrolling. Slide your two fingers up or down on the TouchPad to scroll the selected window or item vertically.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=355\cf0\par
\b Enable horizontal scrolling\b0\par
Select this check box to enable horizontal Virtual Scrolling on windows that have horizontal scroll bars. Sliding your finger back and forth along the lower edge of the TouchPad scrolls the selected window or item horizontally.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=356\cf0\par
\b Enable EdgeMotion when scrolling\b0\par
Select this checkbox if you want scrolling to continue when your fingers reach the edge of the TouchPad. To stop scrolling, lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=357\cf0\par
\b Enable Coasting\b0\par
Coasting helps you scroll easily through long documents. Select this checkbox if you want scrolling to continue after you have lifted your fingers up off the TouchPad surface. To use Coasting, move and lift your fingers in one smooth motion. To stop the coasting motion, place your fingers back down on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=358\cf0\par
\b Practice\b0\par
Click the Practice button to run a simple maze to help you learn how to use scrolling.\par
\par
Before you begin, locate the maze’s start and end points. You cannot see the entire maze at once because it is larger than the window that displays it. Scroll to move your view of the maze in the window. Hold down the Ctrl key and use scrolling to resize the maze.\par
\par
A yellow dot in the upper left corner of the maze marks the entrance. A blue dot on the right side of the maze marks the exit.\par
\par
To practice scrolling:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Click on the red square in the yellow dot.\par
2.\tab Use the arrow keys or tap your TouchPad to navigate through the maze.\par
3.\tab Use the desired scrolling method to move your view of the maze.\par
4.\tab Close the scrolling practice window after you reach the blue dot on the right.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=359\cf0\par
\b Pinch Zoom\b0\par
The Pinch Zoom gesture delivers zooming functionality in many applications. You can use the Pinch Zoom gesture to perform the same functions that a scroll wheel performs on standard Windows applications that support Ctrl-Scroll Wheel zoom functionality.\par
\par
Select the Pinch Zoom checkbox to enable the pinch gesture for zooming.  Watch the video to learn how to perform the gesture.  Use the tips of your fingers for best results.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=360\cf0\par
\b Zoom Rate\b0\par
The Zoom Rate slider controls how quickly the window zooms in response to the Pinch Zoom gesture.\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
To zoom at a slower rate, move the slider toward \b Slow\b0.\par
To zoom at a faster rate, move the slider toward \b Fast\b0.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=361\cf0\par
\b Enable Pivot Rotate\b0\par
When this box is checked, you can rotate an object, such as a photo, in 90 degree increments.\par
\par
Watch the video to learn how to perform this gesture. Ensure that the space between the two fingers remains the same while performing the gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=362\cf0\par
\b Right Corner Pivot\b0\par
To use the Pivot Rotate feature in the lower right corner of the TouchPad, select the Right Corner Pivot checkbox. This may be more comfortable when performing the Pivot Rotate gesture with the left hand.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=363\cf0\par
\b Enable ChiralRotate\b0\par
Check this box to use the ChiralRotate gesture to rotate objects in 90 degree increments. Note that the ChiralRotate gesture works only with supported applications.\par
\par
To use the ChiralRotate gesture:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the left target zone (on the bottom) of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Slide your finger downwards or upwards and begin making a circular motion. To rotate the object in a clockwise direction, move your finger in clockwise circles. To rotate the object in a counterclockwise direction, move your finger in counterclockwise circles.\par
3.\tab Lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface to return to pointing mode.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=364\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Press\b0\par
When this box is checked, a Three-Finger Press will launch a specified application. To customize the application(s) that launch, click the settings button.\par
\par
Watch the video to learn how to perform a Three-Finger Press. Make sure there is a small gap between your fingers when performing this gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=365\cf0\par
\b Touch Sensitivity\b0\par
The Touch Sensitivity slider allows you to control how much finger pressure is required to move the pointer at a particular speed. If you experience erratic pointer movement, try moving the slider toward \b Heavy Touch\b0 . If the pressure is not registering, move the slider towards \b Light Touch\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=366\cf0\par
\b Scrolling\b0\par
The scrolling features allow you to operate the scroll bars of a window without moving the pointer away from your work. The scrolling features work with most scrollable windows such as documents, spreadsheets, and large digital images.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=367\cf0\par
\b Enable reverse scrolling direction\b0\par
This feature allows you to change standard scrolling behavior. Standard scrolling moves the scroll bar in the direction of finger movement. Reversing the scrolling direction moves the document or object in the direction of finger motion.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=368\cf0\par
\b Enable ChiralScrolling\b0\'99\par
This feature enables you to scroll continuously with one, smooth, circular motion. Scrolling speed is controlled by finger speed. To enable this feature, select the Enable ChiralScrolling checkbox.\par
\par
To use ChiralScrolling:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the target zone. To scroll up or down, the target zone is the right side of the TouchPad. To scroll left or right, the target zone is the lower edge of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Move your finger in the direction you want to scroll, and then begin moving your finger in clockwise or counterclockwise circles. Once ChiralScrolling is initiated, your finger can drift anywhere on the TouchPad surface while making a circular motion. Note: To change the direction of scrolling, simply reverse the direction of finger motion. You will begin scrolling in the opposite direction.\par
3.\tab To stop scrolling and return to pointing mode, lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=369\cf0\par
\b Enable EdgeMotion when scrolling\b0\par
Select this checkbox if you want scrolling to continue when your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=370\cf0\par
\b Scrolling Region\b0\par
The scrolling region diagram represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the scrolling regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger's position. If your finger is in an active scrolling region on the TouchPad, the corresponding scrolling area on the diagram will turn a solid color.\par
\par
You can make the scrolling regions larger or smaller by dragging the small black squares on the edge of the cross-hatched area. If you have difficulty triggering scrolling, make the scrolling region larger by moving the edges of the cross-hatched area inward. If you find that scrolling triggers too easily, make the scrolling region smaller.\par
\par
If the scrolling regions are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that vertical and horizontal scrolling are enabled.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=371\cf0\par
\b Rotating\b0\par
The Rotating features provide convenient gestures for rotating photos and other objects.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=372\cf0\par
\b PalmCheck\b0\'99\par
The PalmCheck feature guards against operating the TouchPad with accidental contact. PalmCheck allows the TouchPad to recognize when your palm is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing and ignore that touch. This will help to prevent unwanted pointer movement or clicks.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=373\cf0\par
\b PalmCheck Sensitivity\b0\par
PalmCheck Sensitivity can be adjusted to match your typing and pointing habits.\par
\par
If the TouchPad exhibits undesired pointer movement or clicks, increase the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider toward \b Maximum\b0.\par
\par
If the TouchPad misses intended motions or taps, decrease the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider toward \b Minimum\b0. You are more likely to experience missed motions or taps if the slider is in the red zone.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=374\cf0\par
\b Momentum\b0\'99\par
The Momentum feature delivers smooth, fast, trackball-like mouse movement. One quick flick of the finger on the TouchPad surface can move the cursor across the entire computer screen. Momentum reduces the repetitive motion and fatigue associated with moving the cursor long distances, while providing a smooth and natural feel similar to using a trackball. Momentum is ideal for Real Time Strategy (RTS) games, First Person Shooter (FPS) games, and Role Playing Games (RPG).\par 
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=375\cf0\par
\b Glide distance\b0\par
The Glide distance slider enables you to adjust how far the cursor travels in response to the flick of a finger on the TouchPad.  To stop the cursor, tap once or place your finger down on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
To increase the Glide distance move the slider toward \b Long\b0; to decrease it move the slider toward \b Short\b0.\par
\par
\b Enable bounce off screen edge\b0\par
Select this checkbox to keep the cursor within the boundaries of the computer screen. The bounce off screen edge feature makes it easy to access menu options and controls that are near the edges of the screen.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=376\cf0\par
\b EdgeMotion\b0\'99\par
The EdgeMotion feature is designed to simplify long-distance pointer motions. When your finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad, the pointer will continue to move in the same direction until you lift your finger.\par
\par
The scrolling features also support EdgeMotion. To use EdgeMotion while scrolling, select the \b Enable EdgeMotion when scrolling \b0 checkbox under \b Scrolling\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=377\cf0\par
\b EdgeMotion Region\b0\par
The EdgeMotion Region diagram on the settings window represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the EdgeMotion regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the EdgeMotion Region diagram to show your finger's position. If your finger is in an active EdgeMotion region on the TouchPad, the corresponding EdgeMotion area on the diagram will turn a solid color.\par
\par
You can make the EdgeMotion regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the edge of the cross-hatched area. If you have difficulty triggering EdgeMotion, make the EdgeMotion region larger by moving the edges of the cross-hatched area inward. If EdgeMotion triggers too easily, make the EdgeMotion region smaller.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=378\cf0\par
\b Control speed with finger pressure\b0\par
When this option is selected, the EdgeMotion speed is determined by how hard you press your finger on the TouchPad. Pressing harder will increase the scrolling speed.\par
\par
\b Fixed speed\b0\par
When this option is selected, the pointer always moves at a constant, specified speed when the finger is in the EdgeMotion region.\par
\par

\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=379\cf0\par
\b Accessibility\b0\par
The Accessibility features enable you to specify how the pointer responds to your pointing actions, including keeping the pointer in a window, slowing the speed of movement, or restricting the direction of the pointer movement.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=380\cf0\par
\b Enable sticky borders\b0\par
This option restricts the pointer to the active window. When you try to move the pointer outside the window the first time, it stops at the edge. If you try a second time, the pointer is free to leave the window.\par
\par
Stopping the pointer at the window borders makes it easy to access controls that lie around the edges of a window. For example, to close or resize a window, you can quickly move the pointer in the general direction of the close box or the edge of the window, and the pointer will stop exactly at the edge.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=381\cf0\par
\b Enable slow motion\b0\par
When this option is enabled, you can reduce the speed of the pointer to improve pointing accuracy for fine positioning.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=382\cf0\par
\b Enable constrained motion\b0\par
This feature allows you to force purely horizontal or vertical motion of the pointer on the screen. This feature is most useful when using a drawing program.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=383\cf0\par
\b Tapping\b0\par
When this checkbox is selected, a tap performs the actions of the left mouse button. If you clear this checkbox, the TouchPad buttons are the only way to click.\par
\par
Tapping means quickly touching and then lifting your finger off the TouchPad surface. A light, quick tap works best. Very hard or slow taps are less effective. Tap once for a single click, or twice in rapid succession for a double-click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=384\cf0\par
\b Locking Drags\b0\par
When this feature is selected, objects can be dragged long distances across the screen using several finger strokes. When Locking Drags is selected, a drag action persists after you lift your finger from the TouchPad. Tap the TouchPad again to drop the object and end the drag.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=385\cf0\par
\b Enable Tap Zones\b0\par
When this checkbox is selected, you can assign a specific action or shortcut to each corner of the TouchPad. By tapping in one of the tap zones, the specified action will be performed, usually saving time and clicks.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=386\cf0\par
\b Configure\b0\par
Select an action from the list box to assign it to a tap zone. Many actions do not require additional configuration. However, if more information is required, the Configure button will be enabled. Click the Configure button at any time to view or change the configuration of the action.\par
A variety of actions can be assigned to Tap Zones. Additional actions might be available if you have third-party TouchPad Plug-In software installed. If the list box options are gray and cannot be selected, ensure that \b Enable Tap Zones\b0  checkbox is selected.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=387\cf0\par
\b Buttons\b0\par
By default, the Buttons act as mouse buttons for this pointing device. If this box is not checked, the buttons are disabled and pressing them has no effect; tapping is the only way to click with your pointing device.\par
\par
You can also specify a different action to be performed when you press a button. For example, you could define the right button to "go to a web page in your default browser". Each time you click the right button on your pointing device, your web browser would automatically open and load the specified page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=388\cf0\par
\b Button Action\b0\par
Choose an action from the dropdown menu to assign it to the desired button. The selected action will occur every time you press that button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=389\cf0\par
\b Using the Properties Window\b0\par
The Properties window allows you to personalize your pointing device settings. Use the menu tree to access the customizable features. Click on the arrow next to a feature or double-click on it to see the options.\par
\par
\b Enabling and Disabling Features\b0\par
Check the box to enable a feature; uncheck the box to disable it.\par
\par
\b Settings\b0\par
If the selected feature displays a settings icon that highlights when you hover the pointer over it, you can click it or double-click on the feature's name to configure its settings. If you cannot click a settings icon, ensure the feature’s checkbox and any parent checkboxes are checked.\par
\par
\b Help\b0\par
The question mark icon and Help button can be clicked to display help information.\par
\par
\b Close Button\b0\par
The Close button on the settings window closes the window. The changes will become permanent only after clicking \b Apply\b0  or \b OK\b0  on the Properties window.\par
\par
\b Defaults Button\b0\par
The Defaults button on the Properties window restores all TouchPad settings to the factory-set values.\par
\par
The Defaults button on a settings window returns all the settings in the current window to the factory-set values. The changes will become permanent only after clicking \b Apply\b0  or \b OK\b0  on the Properties window.\par
\par
\b OK Button\b0\par
The OK button saves your changes and closes the Properties window.\par
\par
\b Cancel Button\b0\par
The Cancel button closes the Properties window without saving any changes.\par
\par
\b Apply Button\b0\par
The Apply button saves your changes, but keeps the Properties window open so you can make more changes.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=390\cf0\par
\b Scrolling speed\b0\par
The Scrolling speed slider allows you to adjust the speed of scrolling. Drag the slider to the right for faster scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=391\cf0\par
\b Pointing\b0\par
The Pointing settings pages enable you to specify how the cursor responds to your pointing and selection actions.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=392\cf0\par
\b Touch Sensitivity\b0\par
The Touch Sensitivity slider allows you to adjust how much finger pressure must be applied before the TouchPad responds.\par
\par
If your pointer is moving or clicking erratically, or you work in an area with high humidity, decrease sensitivity by moving the slider toward \b Heavy Touch\b0 .\par
\par
If you have to press too hard to operate the TouchPad or if the TouchPad does not always respond to your taps, increase sensitivity by moving the slider toward \b Light Touch\b0 .\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=393\cf0\par
\b Tap Zones\b0\par
The Tap Zone diagram represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the active Tap Zone regions on the TouchPad surface. When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger's position. If your finger is in an active Tap Zone region on the TouchPad, the corresponding Tap Zone area on the diagram becomes a solid color.\par
\par
You can make the Tap Zone regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the corners of the cross-hatched areas. If you have difficulty triggering a Tap Zone action, make the Tap Zone region larger. If a Tap Zone action triggers too easily, make the Tap Zone region smaller.\par
\par
If the Tap Zones are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that \b Enable Tap Zones\b0  checkbox is selected.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=394\cf0\par
\b Press-to-Select\b0\par
The Press-to-Select feature allows you to use tapping and pressing motions to perform mouse clicks.\par
\par
Tapping or pressing on the tip of the pointing stick is the same as clicking the left mouse button. However, tapping or pressing can be quicker and more convenient than using the mouse button.\par
\par
Tapping means quickly striking and then releasing your finger from the top of the pointing stick. A light, quick tap works best. Very hard or slow taps are less effective. Tap once for a single click, or twice in rapid succession for a double-click.\par
\par
Pressing means swiftly pushing down on the top of the pointing stick with your finger. A light, deliberate press works best. Press straight down to simulate clicking and holding the mouse button. Press straight down and then to the side to click-and-drag in any direction. Release the pressure when you want to release the mouse button.\par
\par
The physical buttons can also be used to click; if you clear this check box, the physical buttons are the only way to click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=395\cf0\par
\b Press to select only\b0\par
When this option is selected, pressing on the tip of the Pointing Stick selects the object, but will not open or move it.\par
\par
\b Press to select drag\b0\par
When this option is selected, pressing on the tip of the pointing stick selects the object and keeps it selected so you can move it with the pointing stick. It is the equivalent of pressing and holding the left mouse button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=396\cf0\par
\b Press-to-Select means Primary Click\b0\par
When this option is selected, pressing on the pointing stick performs the same action as a primary mouse click.\par
\par
\b Press-to-Select means Secondary Click\b0\par
When this option is selected, pressing on the pointing stick performs the same action as a secondary mouse click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=397\cf0\par
\b Pace\b0\par
The Pace slider adjusts how fast you must press on the pointing stick for it to be considered a Press to Select. Adjust the pace slider until your presses register easily. If presses do not occur, move it towards \b Fast\b0. If too many presses occur, move it towards \b Slow\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=398\cf0\par
\b Touch Sensitivity\b0\par
Sensitivity settings allow you to control how much pressure you must exert on the pointing stick to move the pointer at a particular speed.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=399\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Three-Finger Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Three-Finger Flick to browse the Internet or to scroll through photos in a photo viewer window. You can also use the Three-Finger Flick to enter or exit slideshow mode from your photo viewer.  Check this box to enable the use of the Three-Finger Flick for navigation and slideshows.\par
\par
Watch the video to learn how to perform a Three-Finger Flick. Make sure your fingers are slightly separated when you perform this gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=400\cf0\par
Horizontal Three-Finger Flick\par
Slide your fingers to the right or left to page forwards or backwards in a photo viewer or an Internet browser window.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=401\cf0\par
Vertical Three-Finger Flick\par
Slide your fingers upwards to launch slideshow mode. Slide your fingers downwards to exit slideshow mode.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=402\cf0\par
Use the slider to adjust the speed of the EdgeMotion. To increase the speed, move the slider toward \b Fast\b0, and to decrease it, move the slider toward \b Slow\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=403\cf0\par
When Slow Motion is enabled, you can choose a key from the list box that will activate it. To use Slow Motion, press and hold the specified key while moving the pointer.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=404\cf0\par
When constrained motion is enabled, you can choose a key from the list box that will activate it. To use constrained motion, press and hold the specified key while moving the pointer. The initial direction of your pointer motion determines the allowed direction of movement for as long as your finger remains on the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=405\cf0\par
\b Cover Gesture\b0\par
This gesture clears your desktop by minimizing all open windows. Repeating this gesture restores the windows.\par
\par
To use the Cover Gesture:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place your four fingers flat on the TouchPad so that most of it is covered.\par
2.\tab Hold your fingers still until the windows have been minimized or restored.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=406\cf0\par
Smart Check Settings can help reduce unwanted touchpad behavior. See each setting for more detail.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=407\cf0\par
You can press anywhere on the ClickPad to perform a primary, secondary, or middle button click.\par 
\par
By default, pressing one finger down performs a primary click. Pressing two fingers down performs a secondary click. Pressing three fingers down performs a middle button click. Select \b Two-Finger Click \b0 and \b Three-Finger Click \b0 to configure as desired.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=408\cf0\par
To perform a one-finger click, press one finger down on the ClickPad surface until you feel it descend. A one-finger click is equivalent to clicking a primary (left) mouse button.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=409\cf0\par
By default, a one-finger click in the lower right corner of the ClickPad performs the same action as a secondary (right) mouse click.\par
\par
The action performed by a one-finger corner click is configurable. To assign a different action, select an action from the list.\par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear.\par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
If you are in Left-handed configuration, click in the lower left side of the ClickPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=410\cf0\par
To adjust the coasting distance, use the slider. If you want to scroll farther, move the slider towards \b Max\b0 .  To scroll a shorter distance, move the slider towards \b Min\b0 . If the slider does not move, ensure that Enable Coasting is selected.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=411\cf0\par
\b One-Finger Scrolling\b0\par
You can scroll vertically.\par
\par
Click on the window or item you wish to scroll and slide your finger upwards or downwards along the right edge of the TouchPad to scroll vertically.\par
\par
If scrolling does not respond, ensure that the appropriate check box is selected and that the window or item on which you have clicked is scrollable. If you still have problems, check your \b Scrolling Region\b0  settings.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=412\cf0\par
\b ChiralScrolling\b0\par
The ChiralScrolling\'99 feature enables you to scroll continuously with one, smooth, circular motion. Scrolling speed is controlled by finger speed.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable ChiralScrolling\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use ChiralScrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the \i target\i0  zone. To scroll up or down, the target zone is the right side of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Move your finger in the direction you want to scroll, and then begin moving your finger in clockwise or counterclockwise circles. Once ChiralScrolling is initiated, your finger can drift anywhere on the TouchPad surface while making a circular motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
NOTE: To change the direction of scrolling, simply reverse the direction of finger motion. You will begin scrolling in the opposite direction.\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
3.\tab To stop scrolling and return to pointing mode, lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=413\cf0\par
The diagram above represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the scrolling regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
When you place your finger on the TouchPad, arrows appear along the edge of the TouchPad diagram to show your finger's position. If your finger is in an active scrolling region on the TouchPad, the corresponding scrolling area on the diagram will turn a solid color. You can make the scrolling regions larger or smaller by dragging one of the small black squares on the edge of the cross-hatched area.\par
\par
If you have difficulty triggering scrolling, try making the scrolling region larger by moving the edges of the cross-hatched area inward. If you find that scrolling triggers too easily, try making the scrolling region smaller.\par
\par
If the scrolling regions are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that the scrolling feature is enabled.\par
\par
To enable scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select \b Scrolling\b0  in the menu tree to the left.\par
2.\tab Select the desired method of scrolling, and enable vertical scrolling.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=414\cf0\par
\b Buttons\b0\par
By default, the Buttons act as mouse buttons for this pointing device. \par
\par
You can also specify a different action to be performed when you press a button. For example, you could define the right button to "go to a web page in your default browser". Each time you click the right button on your pointing device, your web browser would automatically open and load the specified page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=415\cf0\par
\b Advanced Filters\b0\par
To minimize unintentional pointer motion and tapping, the advanced accidental contact filters require that pointing and tapping begin in the starting zone. The starting zone is a rectangular area in the center of the TouchPad.  The starting zone area can be adjusted by clicking on the Starting Zone Size topic from the menu tree.  To adjust filter activation timing, choose the Filter Activation Time topic from the menu tree.\par
\par
\b Restrict pointing to zone\b0\par
When this option is selected, your finger must be in the starting zone before movement will affect the pointer.  Contact with the TouchPad will be ignored until your finger is in the starting zone.  After pointer motion is initiated, the TouchPad will work normally until the TouchPad is inactive for the specified time.  To adjust filter activation timing, choose the Filter Activation Time topic from the menu tree.\par
\par
\b Restrict tapping to zone\b0\par
When this option is selected, taps must be initiated in the Starting Zone. Taps initiated outside the starting zone will be suppressed. This filter is active only when the TouchPad has been inactive for the specified time. To adjust filter activation timing, choose the Filter Activation Time topic from the menu tree.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=416\cf0\par
The Filter Activation Time slider allows you to adjust when the advanced filters activate.  To increase the length of idle time required to pass before the advanced filters activate, move the slider towards 5 seconds. To decrease the length of idle time required to pass before the advanced filters activate, move the slider towards 0 seconds.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=417\cf0\par
\b PalmCheck-Enhanced\b0\par
Accidental Contact Mitigation Settings can help reduce unwanted touchpad behavior. See each setting for more detail.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=418\cf0\par
The \b PalmCheck\b0\'99 feature guards against operating the TouchPad with accidental contact. PalmCheck allows the TouchPad to recognize when your palm is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing. This will help to prevent unwanted pointer movement or clicks.\par
\par
If the TouchPad exhibits undesired pointer movement or clicks, increase the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider to the right toward \b Max\b0. If the TouchPad misses intended motions or taps, decrease the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider to the left toward \b Off\b0 . You are more likely to experience missed motions or taps if the slider is close to \b Max\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=419\cf0\par
By default, a one-finger click in the lower right corner of the ClickPad 
performs the same action as a secondary(right) mouse click.\par
\par
The action performed by a one-finger corner click is configurable.  To assign a 
different action, select an action from the list.\par
\par
Some actions require additional configuration. If more information is required, 
the \b Configure \b0 button will become enabled or a dialog box will appear.\par
\par
You can click the \b Configure \b0 button at any time to view or change your settings.\par
\par
If you are in Left-handed configuration, click in the lower left side of the 
ClickPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=420\cf0\par
SmartSense is the Accidental Contact Mitigation technology for Synaptics' advanced Image Sensors.  Settings can help reduce unwanted touchpad behavior. See each setting for more detail.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=421\cf0\par
The \b SmartSense \b0 feature guards against operating the TouchPad with accidental contact. SmartSense allows the TouchPad to recognize when your palm is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing. This will help to prevent unwanted pointer movement or clicks.\par
\par
If the TouchPad exhibits undesired pointer movement or clicks, increase the SmartSense setting by moving the slider to the right toward \b Max\b0. If the TouchPad misses intended motions or taps, decrease the SmartSense setting by moving the slider to the left toward \b Off\b0 . You are more likely to experience missed motions or taps if the slider is close to \b Max\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=422\cf0\par
\b Enable Inertia\b0\par
Select this option to continue scrolling after breaking contact with the TouchPad.  Once this is selected, you can enable either \b Coasting\b0  or \b Momentum Scrolling\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=423\cf0\par
\b Enable Momentum Scrolling\b0\par
When this option is selected, use a “flick” motion to begin scrolling, and scrolling will continue after breaking contact with the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=424\cf0\par
To adjust the momentum scrolling distance, use the slider.  If you want to scroll farther, move the slider towards \b Maximum\b0.  To scroll a shorter distance, move the slider towards \b Minimum\b0.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=425\cf0\par
\b Enable Vertical Four-Finger Flick\b0\par
When this option is enabled, a downward or upward four-finger flick minimizes or restores the windows on your desktop. \par
\par
To minimize or restore the windows on your desktop:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place four fingers near the upper or lower edge of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion vertically towards the other edge.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab Perform another Four-Finger Flick to restore the windows on your desktop.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=426\cf0\par
When this option is selected, quickly sliding a finger back and forth across the TouchPad activates a disabled device.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=427\cf0\par
\b Circular Scrolling\b0\par
The Circular Scrolling\'99 feature enables you to scroll continuously with one, smooth, circular motion. Scrolling speed is controlled by finger speed.\par
\par
To enable this feature, select the \b Enable Circular Scrolling\b0  check box.\par
\par
To use Circular Scrolling:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the \i target\i0  zone. To scroll up or down, the target zone is the right side of the TouchPad. To scroll left or right, the target zone is the lower edge of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Move your finger in the direction you want to scroll, and then begin moving your finger in clockwise or counterclockwise circles. Once Circular Scrolling is initiated, your finger can drift anywhere on the TouchPad surface while making a circular motion.\par
\pard\tx180\par
NOTE: To change the direction of scrolling, simply reverse the direction of finger motion. You will begin scrolling in the opposite direction.\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
3.\tab To stop scrolling and return to pointing mode, lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=428\cf0\par
\b Enable Circular Scrolling\b0\'99\par
This feature enables you to scroll continuously with one, smooth, circular motion. Scrolling speed is controlled by finger speed. To enable this feature, select the Enable Circular Scrolling checkbox.\par
\par
To use Circular Scrolling:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place one finger down on the target zone. To scroll up or down, the target zone is the right side of the TouchPad. To scroll left or right, the target zone is the lower edge of the TouchPad.\par
2.\tab Move your finger in the direction you want to scroll, and then begin moving your finger in clockwise or counterclockwise circles. Once Circular Scrolling is initiated, your finger can drift anywhere on the TouchPad surface while making a circular motion. Note: To change the direction of scrolling, simply reverse the direction of finger motion. You will begin scrolling in the opposite direction.\par
3.\tab To stop scrolling and return to pointing mode, lift your finger up off the TouchPad surface.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=429\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Scrolling\b0\par
The Two-Finger Scrolling feature allows you to scroll vertically or horizontally from anywhere on the TouchPad surface. Place two fingers down on the TouchPad and then slide them in the desired direction. You do not need to be in a scrolling region to scroll.\par
\par
Make sure the fingers are slightly apart from each other. Lift both fingers off the TouchPad to stop scrolling.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=430\cf0\par
\b Pinch Zoom\b0\par
The Pinch Zoom gesture delivers zooming functionality in many applications. You can use the Pinch Zoom gesture to perform the same functions that a scroll wheel performs on standard Windows applications that support Ctrl-Scroll Wheel zoom functionality.\par
\par
Select the Pinch Zoom checkbox to enable the pinch gesture for zooming. Use the tips of your fingers for best results.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=431\cf0\par
\b Enable Pivot Rotate\b0\par
When this box is checked, you can rotate an object, such as a photo, in 90 degree increments.\par
\par
Ensure that the space between the two fingers remains the same while performing the gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=432\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Press\b0\par
When this box is checked, a Three-Finger Press will launch a specified application. To customize the application(s) that launch, click the settings button.\par
\par
Make sure there is a small gap between your fingers when performing this gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=433\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Three-Finger Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Three-Finger Flick to browse the Internet or to scroll through photos in a photo viewer window. You can also use the Three-Finger Flick to enter or exit slideshow mode from your photo viewer.  Check this box to enable the use of the Three-Finger Flick for navigation and slideshows.\par
\par
Make sure your fingers are slightly separated when you perform this gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=434\cf0\par
The diagram above represents the surface of the TouchPad. The cross-hatched area on the diagram shows the Right Button Zone on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
You can make the Right Button Zone larger or smaller by dragging the small black square on the corner of the cross-hatched area.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=435\cf0\par
You can press anywhere on the ClickPad to perform a primary, secondary, or middle button click.\par 
\par
By default, pressing one finger down performs a primary click. Pressing one finger in the corner performs a secondary click. Pressing three fingers down performs a middle button click. Select \b Corner Click \b0 and \b Three-Finger Click \b0 to configure as desired.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=436\cf0\par
You can click-and-drag with one finger to move a selected item. \par
\par
\b To perform a One-Finger Click-and-Drag Gesture:\b0\par
1.\tab Use your cursor to highlight the item you want to move.\par
2.\tab Press one finger down on the ClickPad.\par
3.\tab Keep the ClickPad pressed down while dragging the selected item to the desired location.\par
4.\tab Lift your finger off the ClickPad when you want to release the item.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=437\cf0\par
You can click-and-drag with two fingers to perform the equivalent of a right-physical-button click-and-drag.\par
\par
\b To perform a Two-Finger Click-and-Drag Gesture:\b0\par
1.\tab Use your cursor to highlight the item you want to move. \par
2.\tab Press two-fingers down on the ClickPad.\par
3.\tab Keep the ClickPad pressed down while dragging the selected item to the desired location.\par
4.\tab Lift your fingers off the ClickPad when you want to release the item.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=438\cf0\par
You can press anywhere on the ClickPad to perform a primary, secondary, or middle button click.\par 
\par
By default, pressing one finger down performs a primary click. Pressing two fingers down performs a secondary click. Pressing three fingers down performs a middle button click.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=439\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Flick\b0\par
Vertical and horizontal Three-finger flicks provide convenient shortcuts for switching applications and minimizing or restoring your desktop.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=440\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Vertical Flick\b0\par
When this option is enabled, a downward three-finger flick minimizes or restores the windows on your desktop. An upward three-finger flick opens the application switcher.\par
\par
To minimize the windows on your desktop:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers near the upper edge of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion towards the lower edge.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab Repeat the gesture to restore the windows on your desktop.\par
\par
To open the Aero 3D application switcher:\par
\pard\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Place three fingers near the lower edge of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion towards the upper edge.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab The Aero 3D application switcher will open.\par
5.\tab To page through the applications, slide one to three fingers horizontally.\par
6.\tab When the desired application is highlighted, tap on the TouchPad to switch to it.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=441\cf0\par
\b Buttons\b0\par
By default, the Buttons act as mouse buttons for this pointing device. If this box is not checked, the buttons are disabled and pressing them has no effect; tapping is the only way to click with your pointing device.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=442\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Tapping\b0\par
Tap two fingers simultaneously anywhere on the TouchPad to perform the same action as a secondary (right) mouse click. Make sure that there is a slight gap between the fingers when performing a two-finger tap.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=443\cf0\par
\b Prioritize for TrackPoint and keyboard input\b0\par
Prioritizing for TrackPoint and keyboard input will temporarily ignore contact on the touchpad for a short time following input on either the TrackPoint or keyboard.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=444\cf0\par
\b Buttons\b0\par
Adjust button-specific parameters.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=445\cf0\par
\b Right Button Zone\b0\par
Adjust the Right Button Zone size on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=446\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Flick\b0\par
To enable this feature, select Three-Finger Flick.\par
To minimize the windows on your desktop:\par
1.\tab Place three fingers near the upper edge of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion towards the lower edge.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab Repeat the gesture to restore the windows on your desktop.\par
\par
To open the application switcher:\par
1.\tab Place three fingers near the lower edge of the TouchPad. There should be a slight gap between your fingers.\par
2.\tab Flick your fingers in a smooth, straight motion towards the upper edge.\par
3.\tab Lift your fingers off the TouchPad.\par
4.\tab The application switcher will open.\par
5.\tab To page through the applications, slide three fingers horizontally. When the desired application is highlighted, move the cursor to the desired application and click to select it.\par
\par
Horizontal Three-Finger Flick:\par
Horizontal three-finger flicks navigate up and down in a document and forward or back in Windows Explorer. A three-finger flick to the right will navigate forward or page up and a three-finger flick to the left will navigate back or page down.\par 
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=447\cf0\par
\b ASUS Smart Detector\b0\par
Enable the Smart Detector feature to help prevent accidental cursor movement. To activate pointing, press one of the buttons, or begin pointing in the designated Activation Region. While typing, pointing is automatically suppressed unless the pointing begins in the Activation Region.\par
By default the Activation Region is located in the top-center of the TouchPad. To adjust the location of the Active Area, click and drag the circle. To adjust the size of the Active Area, choose one of the pre-set sizes, or choose Customize and specify a size by adjusting the slider.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=448\cf0\par
When \b Restrict contact to zone after typing \b0 is selected, typing will disable the TouchPad until you initiate pointing, tapping, or gestures in the starting zone.\par
After contact is initiated in the starting zone, the TouchPad will work normally until you type again.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=449\cf0\par
\b Adjust EdgeMotion Speed\b0\par
You can choose the EdgeMotion speed by adjusting the slider between \b Slow\b0  and \b Fast\b0 .\par
\par
If the controls on this page are gray and cannot be adjusted, ensure that EdgeMotion is enabled. To enable EdgeMotion, perform the following steps:\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\fi-180\li180\tx180\par
1.\tab Select \b EdgeMotion\b0  in the menu tree to the left.\par
2.\tab Select one of the \b Perform EdgeMotion\b0  options.\par
3.\tab Select \b EdgeMotion Speed\b0  from the menu tree to return to this page.\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=451\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Three-Finger Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Three-Finger Flick to browse the Internet or to scroll through photos in a photo viewer window.\par
\par
Watch the video to learn how to perform a Three-Finger Flick. Make sure your fingers are slightly separated when you perform this gesture.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=452\cf0\par
\b Locking Drags\b0\par
When this feature is selected, objects can be dragged long distances across the screen using several finger strokes. When Locking Drags is selected, a drag action persists for a short time after you lift your finger from the TouchPad. Tap the TouchPad again to drop the object and end the drag.\par
\par
The locking drag will automatically release if your finger does not immediately touch the TouchPad again.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=453\cf0\par
\b Using the Properties Window\b0\par
The Properties window allows you to personalize your pointing device settings. Use the menu tree to access the customizable features. Click on the arrow next to a feature or double-click on it to see the options.\par
\par
\b Enabling and Disabling Features\b0\par
Check the box to enable a feature; uncheck the box to disable it.\par
\par
\b Settings\b0\par
If the selected feature displays a settings icon that highlights when you hover the pointer over it, you can click it or double-click on the feature's name to configure its settings. If you cannot click a settings icon, ensure the feature’s checkbox and any parent checkboxes are checked.\par
\par
\b Help\b0\par
The question mark icon and Help button can be clicked to display help information.\par
\par
\b Close Button\b0\par
The Close button on the settings window closes the window. The changes will become permanent only after clicking \b Apply\b0  or \b OK\b0  on the Properties window.\par
\par
\b Defaults Button\b0\par
The Defaults button on the Properties window restores all TouchPad settings to the factory-set values, except for the Three-Finger Press value. The value for Three-Finger Press is not changed.\par
\par
The Defaults button on a settings window returns all the settings in the current window to the factory-set values. The changes will become permanent only after clicking \b Apply\b0  or \b OK\b0  on the Properties window.\par
\par
\b OK Button\b0\par
The OK button saves your changes and closes the Properties window.\par
\par
\b Cancel Button\b0\par
The Cancel button closes the Properties window without saving any changes.\par
\par
\b Apply Button\b0\par
The Apply button saves your changes, but keeps the Properties window open so you can make more changes.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=454\cf0\par
\b Enable Edge Swipes\b0\par
Select the Enable Edge Swipes checkbox to enable a one-finger movement from the edge of the TouchPad in towards the center of the TouchPad:\par
\par
1.\tab A swipe from the left edge to the center switches from the currently open application to the previously open application.\par
2.\tab A swipe from the right edge to the center opens or closes the Charms bar.\par
3.\tab A swipe from the the top edge to the center opens or closes the Application Bar.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=455\cf0\par
To perform a two-finger click, press two fingers down on the ClickPad surface until you feel it descend. By default, a two-finger click is equivalent to clicking a secondary button. \par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=456\cf0\par
\b Enable Reverse Three-Finger Flick direction\b0\par
When this option is selected, the direction of the Three-Finger flick reverses. For example, a Three-Finger flick to the left would navigate to the next page and a Three-Finger Flick to the right would navigate to the previous page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=457\cf0\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=500\cf0\par
\b Click\b0\par
Place one finger on the TouchPad, press and release to generate a click. Click in the bottom right corner of the TouchPad for a right button action (usually to bring up context menus) and click in other areas for a left button action (usually select action). In left handed usage mode, use the left bottom corner to bring up context menus.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=501\cf0\par
\b Tap\b0\par
Quickly touch and lift one or more fingers on the TouchPad. A tap on the TouchPad works the same as a click action.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=502\cf0\par
\b Drag & Drop\b0\par
To drag an object, tap twice on the object and keep the finger down on the TouchPad on the second tap. Move the finger to move the object to the desired location:\par
1. A Holding drag drops the object after the finger is lifted.\par
2. A Locking drag requires an additional tap to drop the object.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=503\cf0\par
\b Edge Scroll\b0\par
Swipe a finger “along” the right edge or bottom edge of the TouchPad to perform vertical and horizontal scroll action respectively. Adjust the Scrolling Speed slider to control the scrolling speed relative to finger movement. Continuous scrolling lets the scrolling continue when the swiping finger reaches an edge if the finger is held stationary without having to swipe further.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=504\cf0\par
\b Edge Swipe\b0\par
With one smooth motion of a finger, swipe “across” a top or side edge of the TouchPad to generate a Window 8 “edge pull.” Swiping across left edge switches applications, across right edge brings up charms menu and across top brings up application bar.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=505\cf0\par
\b Buttons\b0\par
Press the left button to select an item and press the right button to display the context menu. In left-handed usage mode, the button actions are swapped.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=506\cf0\par
\b Click\b0\par
Place two fingers on the TouchPad, press and release to bring up the context menu. This action is the same as a click in the bottom right corner of the TouchPad.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=507\cf0\par
\b Scroll\b0\par
Place two fingers slightly apart on the TouchPad and move them in unison in a smooth vertical or horizontal direction to scroll a document.\par
\par 
Adjust the Scrolling Speed slider to control the scrolling speed relative to finger movement.\par
\par
Continuous scrolling lets the scrolling continue when the swiping finger reaches an edge if the finger is held stationary without having to swipe further.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=508\cf0\par
\b Zoom\b0\par
Place two fingers on the TouchPad and move them towards or away from each other to zoom out (make the visible items smaller, view more items on the page) or zoom in (make visible items larger). Use the slider to control the speed of zooming relative to finger movement.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=509\cf0\par
\b Rotate\b0\par
Place two fingers slightly apart on the TouchPad. Slide the fingers in a clockwise or counterclockwise direction. The motion is similar to turning a knob.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=510\cf0\par
\b Click\b0\par
Place three fingers on the TouchPad, press and release to launch mission control.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=511\cf0\par
\b Swipe/Flick\b0\par
Place three fingers on the TouchPad. Move them swiftly in a horizontal or vertical direction and lift to perform actions on the active application.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=512\cf0\par
\b Swipe/Flick\b0\par
Place four fingers on the TouchPad. Move them swiftly in a horizontal or vertical direction and lift to navigate between open applications.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=513\cf0\par
\b Palm Tracking\b0\par
If the TouchPad registers undesired pointer movement or taps during typing, move the slider toward Maximum. The Maximum setting suppresses almost all unintended palm contact, but may also suppress intended finger contact.\par
\par
If the TouchPad misses intended gestures or taps, move the slider toward Minimum.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=514\cf0\par
\b Sensitivity\b0\par
Adjust the slider to control the finger pressure needed for the TouchPad to respond to finger movement. High sensitivity can detect very light contact, but also increases the chance of unintended cursor motion and selection.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=515\cf0\par
\b Edge Motion\b0\par
While moving the cursor, when the finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad, the cursor will continue to move in the direction of motion at a constant speed by simply holding the finger at the edge. Adjust the slider to control the speed of cursor motion.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=516\cf0\par
\b TouchPad Status\b0\par
If the TouchPad is disabled, it can be made to re-enable after a system reboot or continue to remain disabled unless explicitly re-enabled.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=517\cf0\par
\b Three-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Three-Finger Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Three-Finger Flick left/right to browse the Internet or to scroll through photos in a photo viewer window.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=518\cf0\par
\b Four-Finger Flick\b0\par
The Four-Finger Flick gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. You can use the Four-Finger Flick left/right to browse the Internet or to scroll through photos in a photo viewer window.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=519\cf0\par
\b Enable reverse scrolling direction\b0\par
This feature allows you to change standard scrolling behavior. Standard scrolling moves the document or object in the direction of finger motion. Reversing the scrolling direction moves the scroll bar in the direction of finger movement.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=520\cf0\par
\b Disable TouchPad when an external mouse is connected\b0\par
After you connect an external mouse, you can select this option to disable the TouchPad. To enable the TouchPad again, deselect this option.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=521\cf0\par
\b Enable Reverse Three-Finger Flick direction\b0\par
When this option is selected, the direction of the Three-Finger flick reverses. For example, a Three-Finger flick to the left would navigate to the previous page and a Three-Finger Flick to the right would navigate to the next page.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=522\cf0\par
\par
\b Synaptics Trademarks\b0\par
ChiralMotion and design (stylized)™, ChiralMotion Logo™, ChiralMotion®, ClearButtons™, ClearPad™, ClickButtons™, ClickEQ Logo™, ClickEQ™, ClickPad™, ClickSmart™, ClickZones™, DesignSafe™, Design Studio™, DisplayPad™, DualMode™, DualPointing™, EdgeMotion™, EGR-Enhanced Gesture Recognition™, EGR™, Enhanced Gesture Recognition™, FlexPad™, ForcePad™, InterTouch™, Momentum™, NavPoint®, OTLIB™, PalmCheck™, PanelPort™, ProductionSafe™, QuickStroke®, SafePass™, ScrollStrip™, Sensitivity Tuning Wizard™, SGS™, SignalClarity™, SmartSense™, Synaptics | Scrybe™, Synaptics Gesture Suite™, Synaptics OneTouch™, Synaptics®, The Synaptics Logo®, The Synaptics OneTouch Logo (stylized)™, The Synaptics Scrybe Logo™, ThinTouch™, TouchButtons™, TouchPad™, TouchStyk™, ViewXpand™.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=523\cf0\par
\b One-Finger Gestures\b0\par
One-Finger Gestures such as pointing, tapping, button clicking, can provide essential input function or shortcuts to frequently used applications or functions. Click on the help for the individual gestures to find out what they do.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=524\cf0\par
\b Additional Features\b0\par
There are some advance features can be adjusted by user to provide better user experience. Click on the help of the individual item for more reference.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=525\cf0\par
You can press anywhere on the ClickPad to perform a primary click or a secondary click.\par 
\par
By default, pressing one finger down performs a primary click. Pressing one finger in the corner performs a secondary click. Select \b Corner Click \b0 to configure as desired.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=526\cf0\par
This option restricts the pointer to the active window. When you try to move the pointer outside the window the first time, it stops at the edge. If you try a second time, the pointer is free to leave the window.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=527\cf0\par
When the finger reaches the edge of the TouchPad, the cursor will continue to move in the direction of motion at a constant speed by simply holding the finger at the edge.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=528\cf0\par
Adjust the slider to control the speed of the continued cursor motion. To increase the speed, move the slider toward Fast, and to decrease it, move the slider toward Slow.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=529\cf0\par
This diagram represents the surface of the Touchpad. The lined area on the diagram shows the EdgeMotion regions on the TouchPad surface.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=530\cf0\par
When this option is selected, you can assign a specific action or shortcut to each corner of the TouchPad.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=531\cf0\par
Adjust the slider to control the finger pressure needed for the TouchPad to respond to finger movement. High sensitivity can detect very light contact, but also increases the chance of unintended cursor motion and selection.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=532\cf0\par
Momentum can reduces the repetitive motion and fatigue associated with moving the cursor long distances by increasing the distance the cursor travels with a finger flick.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=533\cf0\par
PalmCheck allows the TouchPad to recognize when your palm is resting on it or brushing its surface while you are typing and ignore that touch.\par
\par
If the TouchPad exhibits undesired pointer movement or clicks, increase the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider toward Maximum. If the TouchPad misses intended motions or taps, decrease the PalmCheck setting by moving the slider toward Minimum. You are more likely to experience missed motions or taps if the slider is close to Max.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=534\cf0\par
With the Enable edge tap filter option selected, incidental finger tapping along the edges of the TouchPad will be ignored. \par
\par
The edge region is denoted by the lined area. You can adjust the size of the edge region by dragging the black resize handles. 
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=535\cf0\par
When enabled, this filter helps to predict when you are intentionally using Pinch Zoom, Two-Finger Scroll, Rotate, and Application Gestures. If initial contact occurs outside the lined area, the gesture will be ignored.\par
\par
The area of the pad that will allow gesture use is denoted by the lined area. You can adjust the size of this area by dragging the black resize handles.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=536\cf0\par
Slide a finger “across” a top or side edge of the TouchPad to generate an edge swipe. Swiping across the left edge switches applications, across the right edge brings up the charms menu and across the top brings up the application menu.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=537\cf0\par
Use the Pinch Zoom gesture to perform the same functions that a scroll wheel performs on standard Windows applications that support Ctrl-Scroll Wheel zoom functionality. Place two fingers on the TouchPad and slide them closer together to zoom in and slide them farther apart to zoom out.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=538\cf0\par
The Rotate gesture enables you to rotate items such as photos, PDF files, or other items. Select the object to rotate, place two fingers on the TouchPad and slide your fingers clockwise or counterclockwise to turn the object accordingly.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=539\cf0\par
The Three Finger Swipe gesture enhances navigation for a variety of applications. The horizontal swipe navigates forward and back in a browser or when paging through pictures. In some applications, the vertical swipe up enters full screen mode and a swipe down exits full screen to normal view.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=540\cf0\par
Place four fingers on the TouchPad. Move them swiftly in a horizontal or vertical direction and lift to navigate between open applications.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=541\cf0\par
Drag the scrolling speed slider to the right for faster scrolling.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=542\cf0\par
Inertia continues scrolling after your finger leaves the TouchPad. To scroll a shorter distance, move the slider towards Min. To scroll farther, move the slider towards Max. When the slider is on Max, scrolling will continue after your finger leaves the TouchPad until you place your finger back on the surface.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=543\cf0\par
The Two-Finger Scrolling feature allows you to scroll vertically or horizontally from anywhere on the TouchPad surface. Place two fingers down on the TouchPad and then slide them in the desired direction. You do not need to be in a scrolling region to scroll.\par
\par
Make sure the fingers are slightly apart from each other. Lift both fingers off the TouchPad to stop scrolling.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=544\cf0\par
Click on the window or item you wish to scroll and slide your finger upwards or downwards along the right edge of the TouchPad to scroll vertically. Slide your finger to the left or right along the lower edge of the TouchPad to scroll horizontally.\par
\par
If scrolling does not respond, ensure that scrolling is enabled and that the window or item on which you have clicked is scrollable. If you still have problems, check your Scrolling Region settings.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=545\cf0\par
The shaded area on the diagram shows the scrolling regions on the TouchPad surface.\par
\par
If you have difficulty triggering scrolling, try choosing a larger scrolling region. If you find that scrolling triggers too easily, try choosing a smaller scrolling region.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=546\cf0\par
A tap on the TouchPad works the same as a click. Tap once for a single click, or twice in rapid succession for a double-click.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=547\cf0\par
Place one or more fingers on the TouchPad, press and release. A one finger click in the bottom corner performs the action of a right click.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=548\cf0\par
\b Adjust Pointer Motion Speed\b0\par
Choose the pointer speed by adjusting the slider between Slow and Fast.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=549\cf0\par
\b Tapping\b0\par
When enabled, a tap performs the actions of the left mouse button. Tap once for a single click, or twice in rapid succession for a double-click.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=550\cf0\par
\b MultiFinger Gestures\b0\par
MultiFinger Gestures provide shortcuts to frequently used applications or functions. The computer’s response to these gestures can be application-specific. 
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=551\cf0\par
\b Clicking:\b0\par
The ForcePad detects when you apply pressure to it, so pressing on the ForcePad is like clicking a mouse button. To make an on-screen selection you can use the “click” function by applying pressure to your ForcePad.
 Press down with one finger anywhere on the ForcePad to perform a primary click. To perform a secondary click, press down with one finger in the lower right corner or with two fingers anywhere on the ForcePad.
\par
\par
Use the Volume Mixer in your system tray (speaker icon) to control the volume of the click sound. 
\par
\par
Select the object you want to drag by pressing on the ForcePad and moving the object slightly. After this initial move, you can release the force and move the object. The object will be dropped when you lift your finger off the ForcePad.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=552\cf0\par
\b Force Click Threshold slider:\b0\par
The amount of force needed to register a click can by adjusted by using the Force click threshold slider. To require more force to click, move the slider towards Maximum, to require less force, move it towards Minimum.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=553\cf0\par
You can click-and-drag with one finger to move a selected item. You can click-and-drag with two fingers to perform the equivalent of a right-physical-button click-and-drag.\par
\par
\b To perform a One-Finger Click-and-Drag Gesture:\b0\par
1.\tab Use your cursor to highlight the item you want to move.\par
2.\tab Press one finger down on the TouchPad.\par
3.\tab Keep the TouchPad pressed down while dragging the selected item to the desired location.\par
4.\tab Lift your finger off the TouchPad when you want to release the item.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\b To perform a Two-Finger Click-and-Drag Gesture:\b0\par
1.\tab Use your cursor to highlight the item you want to move. \par
2.\tab Press two-finger down on the TouchPad.\par
3.\tab Keep the TouchPad pressed down while dragging the selected item to the desired location.\par
4.\tab Lift your fingers off the ClickPad when you want to release the item.\par
\pard\tx180\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=554\cf0\par
\b Two-Finger Scrolling\b0\par
The Two-Finger Scrolling feature allows you to scroll vertically or horizontally from anywhere on the TouchPad surface. Place two fingers down on the TouchPad and then slide them in the desired direction. You do not need to be in a scrolling region to scroll.
\par
Watch the video to learn how to use Two-Finger Scrolling. Make sure the fingers are slightly apart from each other. Lift both fingers off the TouchPad to stop scrolling.
\par
\b Force-enhanced gesture:\b0\par
After scrolling is initiated, press down on the TouchPad and keep your fingers stationary to continue scrolling. Increase the pressure to go faster and decrease the pressure to go slower. To stop scrolling, release the pressure, or lift your fingers from the TouchPad.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=555\cf0\par
\b Pinch Zoom\b0\par
The Pinch Zoom gesture delivers zooming functionality in many applications. You can use the Pinch Zoom gesture to perform the same functions that a scroll wheel performs on standard Windows applications that support Ctrl-Scroll Wheel zoom functionality.
\par
Select the Pinch Zoom checkbox to enable the pinch gesture for zooming.  Watch the video to learn how to perform the gesture.  Use the tips of your fingers for best results.
\par
\b Force-enhanced gesture:\b0\par
After zooming is initiated, press down on the TouchPad and keep your fingers stationary to continue zooming that direction. Increase the pressure to go faster and decrease the pressure to go slower. To stop zooming, release the pressure, or lift your fingers from the TouchPad.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=556\cf0\par
\b Scrolling\b0\par
The scrolling features allow you to operate the scroll bars of a window without moving the pointer away from your work. The scrolling features work with most scrollable windows such as documents, spreadsheets, and large digital images.
\par
\b Force-enhanced gesture:\b0\par
After scrolling is initiated, press down on the TouchPad and keep your fingers stationary to continue scrolling. Increase the pressure to go faster and decrease the pressure to go slower. To stop scrolling, release the pressure, or lift your fingers from the TouchPad.
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=557\cf0\par
\b Enable Edge Swipes\b0\par
Select the Enable Edge Swipes checkbox to enable a one-finger movement from the edge of the TouchPad in towards the center of the TouchPad:\par
\par
A swipe from the left edge to the center switches from the currently open application to the previously open application.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=558\cf0\par
\b Enable Edge Swipes\b0\par
Select the Enable Edge Swipes checkbox to enable a one-finger movement from the edge of the TouchPad in towards the center of the TouchPad:\par
\par
A swipe from the right edge to the center opens or closes the Charms bar.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=559\cf0\par
\b Enable Edge Swipes\b0\par
Select the Enable Edge Swipes checkbox to enable a one-finger movement from the edge of the TouchPad in towards the center of the TouchPad:\par
\par
A swipe from the the top edge to the center opens or closes the Application Bar.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=560\cf0\par
To enable one-finger click across the entire ClickPad surface, select the check box marked "Enable clicks on the pointer control surface." When you want to disable clicks on the ClickPad surface, deselect the check box. When disabled, only clicks on the right and left buttons are enabled.\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=561\cf0\par
When you want to use the entire ClickPad surface to control the pointer, select this check box. When you want to disable pointing in the right and left button zones, deselect the check box.  When deselected, you cannot use the right and left button zones to control the pointer.\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=562\cf0\par
The \b Accessibility\b0  features enable you to specify how the cursor responds to your pointing actions.\par
\par
\cf1 ##HelpTopicID=563\cf0\par
}

